],"sha":"d564198103b6dd4516edb6b3eba67330ad2c1199","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/d564198103b6dd4516edb6b3eba67330ad2c1199"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/ee485e51dea361172d5450ef3f265afe6d106673/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T11:15:06Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T11:15:06Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Remove the local Docker execution lane (#675)\n\n* Run the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward\n\nThe one end-to-end test of the whole stack ran its campaign through a local Docker\nservice started by the script. It now deploys the service into a kind cluster on the\nrunner -- one node, hostPath directories, the same shape as a developer''s minikube -- and\ndrives the same five steps through a port-forward on the conventional port. The cluster\nlane runs only inside a cluster, since a campaign''s pods deliver their outputs to the\nservice over the cluster network, so the service is deployed in rather than pointed at.\n\nWhat the cluster changes about the test, and how each is met: the deployment''s data root\nis a directory on the node, so a runner directory is mounted there and the results are\nread from the host as before; one deployment serves every workflow of a run, so a\nworkflow now returns its campaign''s directory and the packing comparison is handed the\ntwo it should compare rather than finding \"the\" campaign under a root; the cluster lane\nnamespaces a job''s parameter file by batch, so the comparison skips that file by its\nshape; the kubelet has no registry login, so setup is given the runner''s and turns it into\nthe pull secret every pod uses; the deployment mints its own access token, which is read\nback with `vast service token` and never echoed, since a login prints it in the curl line\nit suggests; and the results arrive owned by the pods'' user, so the retrigger step takes\nownership before staging beside them.\n\nThe job now needs the sidecar and controller images of this run as well as the base,\nbecause the service is one of them and its pods transfer with the other.\n\n* Install kind with the runner''s privileges: its bin directory is not the runner''s to chmod\n\n* The end-to-end job installs the cluster lane, which is its own distribution\n\n* A lane that runs nothing, for the tests of what every lane shares\n\nMost of the service is correct for any lane -- workspaces, files, records, listings, the\nevent log, imports, the routes and the MCP tools over them -- and a test of that code needs\na concrete class to construct, not a lane. Those tests constructed the local Docker lane,\nbecause it was the implementation that needed no cluster. They now construct NullLane, a\nServiceBase that answers every hook the base leaves to a lane in the way that commits to\nnothing: a launch is admitted and reaches the controller, which the tests that launch\npatch out; nothing is built, nothing runs, no job exists, no resources are measured, and\nevery operation that would need a driver is refused by name, exactly as a lane that lacks\nit would refuse it. A test that needs one answer replaces that one hook.\n\nSo what these tests exercise is the base''s own code, and what they no longer depend on is\na lane. The tests that are about the local lane itself are untouched here and go with it.\nTwo tests said which postprocess mode they meant by relying on the local lane''s; they say\nit directly now.\n\n* Remove the local Docker execution lane\n\nA service runs the campaigns'' containers where it is deployed, and that is a Kubernetes\ncluster: on one machine a minikube or kind cluster, deployed with the same command and\nthe one-node configuration an operator uses for a team. The lane that ran docker compose\nbeside the service process is gone with everything that existed only for it: the\ntransport, the compose script generator, the Docker exec lane, the local job log, the\nhost display, the local image store, the Docker backend, the lane''s entry point, and the\nend-to-end test''s local service. The end-to-end test runs against a cluster.\n\nWhat the lane alone offered goes with it rather than being kept as an option nothing\nanswers: show_gui on a launch and on an exec, the execution.local parameter overrides it\nstaged, the tarball-on-disk share, the archive directory, the in-process postprocessing\nbranch of the worker. A request for any of them is refused by the schema or the CLI\nrather than accepted and ignored.\n\nThe service half keeps its shape. ServiceBase stays the abstract half with the cluster\nlane as its production implementer and the suite''s null lane as the other, which is what\nlets the base''s own code be tested without a cluster; the hooks a lane answers are\nunchanged. vast serve resolves the lane it runs by name through the entry-point group as\nbefore, needs no --backend when exactly one is installed, and refuses to start with none,\nnaming the distribution that ships one: a core alone serves nothing and says so. The\nclient factory refuses an empty URL unconditionally, and every MCP tool reports no\nservice where it used to read this host''s disk instead.\n\nTwo things the deletion surfaced are fixed in the lane that stays. An archive no\ndeployment could import back was refused only by the deleted backend; the check is a\nmodule function the cluster lane now calls before a byte is read. And the reserved\nenvironment names were checked against the deleted emitter only, so the cluster''s\nNODE_NAME, hashed into a run''s provenance, was never reserved.\n\nCompat-Unchanged: a comment in entrypoint.sh says where NODE_NAME comes from; nothing the host relies on moved\n\n* Docs and agent rules for one execution lane\n\nThe pages that described running a service on this machine over Docker now describe the\none-node deployment: minikube or kind, vast cluster setup with the minikube\nconfiguration, a port-forward on the conventional port. The deployment page has one\ndeployment and an access matrix over how it is reached; the architecture page''s lane\nsections become \"A lane refuses by name\" and \"One base, the lane''s hooks\"; the\nconfiguration page loses the local block and the per-lane asymmetries of shared memory,\ntimeouts, resources and sizing; the developer guide''s first loop is workspace init, run,\nwait, download.\n\nAGENTS.md states the rule for one lane: every operation is on the interface, the lane\nanswers it or refuses it by name, and what is correct for any lane lives on the base\nwith the null lane as its second implementer.\n\n* Comments and docstrings describe one lane\n\nWhat the code says about itself still contrasted two lanes: what \"the local lane\" did with\na bind mount, a compose step, a shell script or the host''s own counters, and what \"both\nlanes\" shared. Each sentence now describes the code as it is: one lane, a base that is\ncorrect for any lane, and the development-machine tools (vast configuration generate,\nvast results postprocess) that run a container through docker where a pod is not\navailable. Prose only; no behaviour changes.\n\n* The OpenAPI snapshot follows the reworded field docs\n\nThe field comments on the interface models are the schema''s descriptions, so the\ncommitted snapshot and the generated UI types move with them.\n\n* Read a recorded image''s labels and build lock from its registry, never from docker\n\nThe probes that shelled out to docker -- an image''s labels, its build lock, its local id\nand revision -- run in the service and the controller, whose images have no docker CLI,\nso they could only answer \"cannot tell\". They are removed.\n\nThe retrigger pre-flight asks the service for them instead: two hooks on ServiceBase,\nanswered by the cluster service through the registry with the deployment''s pull\ncredential, the reader a launch already uses. The host axis reads the protocol label from\nthere and reports an image the registry would not answer for as unknown, with that\nreason. The controller reads a build lock through its backend alone, and the controller''s\nbranches for a backend with no cluster configuration, which no backend is, are removed.\n\n* Refuse execution.local and unknown launch-request fields rather than ignoring them\n\nA .vast declaring execution.local validated and was silently dropped, since the execution\nschema ignores undeclared keys; the overrides it holds apply to no run. It is now refused\nwhen authoring and dropped with a warning when an archived campaign is read, as the other\narchived-only keys are. The config version stays at 4.\n\nCreateCampaignRequest and ExecRequest refuse a field they do not declare, so a request\ncarrying show_gui or a backend selector is answered with the field''s name instead of being\naccepted and ignored. The container CLI no longer sends backend. The OpenAPI snapshot and\nthe web UI''s generated types follow.\n\n* Name the service''s parts for what they are rather than for a lane\n\nWith one execution runtime there is no lane to name. The identifiers that carried the word\nsay what they are instead: UnsupportedOperation (wire code unsupported_operation) names the\nimplementation that declines, each implementation''s IMPLEMENTATION attribute says which it\nis, the test double is NullService, and the held-container runner is ExecRunner /\nKubeExecRunner. The MCP validation tools report where they checked as \"source\", and a\ncampaign record carries its execution_type under that name.\n\nThe serve command takes no --backend. It serves the one implementation installed through\nthe robovast.execution_backends entry point, and refuses to start with none installed,\nnaming robovast-cluster, or with several.\n\n* Describe one runtime in comments, docs and tests\n\nComments, docstrings, docs, CLI help, MCP tool descriptions and test names describe the\nservice as it runs, in a cluster, instead of contrasting it with a second runtime. Sentences\nthat only drew that contrast are gone, and the architecture pages describe the design as it\nis: ServiceBase with ClusterService as its implementation and NullService for tests, an\noperation an implementation does not offer refused with UnsupportedOperation, and the\nimplementation resolved through its entry point.\n\nTests named after the contrast are named after what they hold. The web UI''s generated API\ntypes follow the reworded docstrings.\n\n* The end-to-end retrigger check reads the recorded image from its registry\n\nThe pre-flight takes the readers for an image''s labels and build lock, so the CI script\nhands it the registry client''s, which answer for the public images the run pushed.\n\n* Remove what only a service outside the cluster could reach\n\nThe serve command runs only inside a cluster, so the code that answered for a service on a\nworkstation had no caller left:\n\n- Pinned workspaces: the serve command''s --workspace-dir, and the workspace registry''s\n  read-only in-place entry with the refusals that guarded it.\n- VersionInfo.results_root and sources_root, always null, with the route''s redaction of them.\n- describe_world''s backend argument and ExecRunner.exec_in''s env argument, both accepted and\n  ignored.\n- The on-disk campaign tarball writer, the text renderer of the code-provenance record, a\n  scheduling hint nothing raised, a duplicated helper, and the usage sampler''s priming read.\n\nScene generation and screenshots always run through the implementation''s runner, which\n_scene_runner_context now must provide. Building a workspace''s scene passes it too, instead\nof falling back to a docker run the service cannot perform.","tree":{"sha":"59a90648b50da6b8607b9b4c6c22da35a25138be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/59a90648b50da6b8607b9b4c6c22da35a25138be"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/ee485e51dea361172d5450ef3f265afe6d106673","verification":{"payload":"tree 59a90648b50da6b8607b9b4c6c22da35a25138be\nparent a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790248506 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790248506 +0200\n\nRemove the local Docker execution lane (#675)\n\n* Run the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward\n\nThe one end-to-end test of the whole stack ran its campaign through a local Docker\nservice started by the script. It now deploys the service into a kind cluster on the\nrunner -- one node, hostPath directories, the same shape as a developer''s minikube -- and\ndrives the same five steps through a port-forward on the conventional port. The cluster\nlane runs only inside a cluster, since a campaign''s pods deliver their outputs to the\nservice over the cluster network, so the service is deployed in rather than pointed at.\n\nWhat the cluster changes about the test, and how each is met: the deployment''s data root\nis a directory on the node, so a runner directory is mounted there and the results are\nread from the host as before; one deployment serves every workflow of a run, so a\nworkflow now returns its campaign''s directory and the packing comparison is handed the\ntwo it should compare rather than finding \"the\" campaign under a root; the cluster lane\nnamespaces a job''s parameter file by batch, so the comparison skips that file by its\nshape; the kubelet has no registry login, so setup is given the runner''s and turns it into\nthe pull secret every pod uses; the deployment mints its own access token, which is read\nback with `vast service token` and never echoed, since a login prints it in the curl line\nit suggests; and the results arrive owned by the pods'' user, so the retrigger step takes\nownership before staging beside them.\n\nThe job now needs the sidecar and controller images of this run as well as the base,\nbecause the service is one of them and its pods transfer with the other.\n\n* Install kind with the runner''s privileges: its bin directory is not the runner''s to chmod\n\n* The end-to-end job installs the cluster lane, which is its own distribution\n\n* A lane that runs nothing, for the tests of what every lane shares\n\nMost of the service is correct for any lane -- workspaces, files, records, listings, the\nevent log, imports, the routes and the MCP tools over them -- and a test of that code needs\na concrete class to construct, not a lane. Those tests constructed the local Docker lane,\nbecause it was the implementation that needed no cluster. They now construct NullLane, a\nServiceBase that answers every hook the base leaves to a lane in the way that commits to\nnothing: a launch is admitted and reaches the controller, which the tests that launch\npatch out; nothing is built, nothing runs, no job exists, no resources are measured, and\nevery operation that would need a driver is refused by name, exactly as a lane that lacks\nit would refuse it. A test that needs one answer replaces that one hook.\n\nSo what these tests exercise is the base''s own code, and what they no longer depend on is\na lane. The tests that are about the local lane itself are untouched here and go with it.\nTwo tests said which postprocess mode they meant by relying on the local lane''s; they say\nit directly now.\n\n* Remove the local Docker execution lane\n\nA service runs the campaigns'' containers where it is deployed, and that is a Kubernetes\ncluster: on one machine a minikube or kind cluster, deployed with the same command and\nthe one-node configuration an operator uses for a team. The lane that ran docker compose\nbeside the service process is gone with everything that existed only for it: the\ntransport, the compose script generator, the Docker exec lane, the local job log, the\nhost display, the local image store, the Docker backend, the lane''s entry point, and the\nend-to-end test''s local service. The end-to-end test runs against a cluster.\n\nWhat the lane alone offered goes with it rather than being kept as an option nothing\nanswers: show_gui on a launch and on an exec, the execution.local parameter overrides it\nstaged, the tarball-on-disk share, the archive directory, the in-process postprocessing\nbranch of the worker. A request for any of them is refused by the schema or the CLI\nrather than accepted and ignored.\n\nThe service half keeps its shape. ServiceBase stays the abstract half with the cluster\nlane as its production implementer and the suite''s null lane as the other, which is what\nlets the base''s own code be tested without a cluster; the hooks a lane answers are\nunchanged. vast serve resolves the lane it runs by name through the entry-point group as\nbefore, needs no --backend when exactly one is installed, and refuses to start with none,\nnaming the distribution that ships one: a core alone serves nothing and says so. The\nclient factory refuses an empty URL unconditionally, and every MCP tool reports no\nservice where it used to read this host''s disk instead.\n\nTwo things the deletion surfaced are fixed in the lane that stays. An archive no\ndeployment could import back was refused only by the deleted backend; the check is a\nmodule function the cluster lane now calls before a byte is read. And the reserved\nenvironment names were checked against the deleted emitter only, so the cluster''s\nNODE_NAME, hashed into a run''s provenance, was never reserved.\n\nCompat-Unchanged: a comment in entrypoint.sh says where NODE_NAME comes from; nothing the host relies on moved\n\n* Docs and agent rules for one execution lane\n\nThe pages that described running a service on this machine over Docker now describe the\none-node deployment: minikube or kind, vast cluster setup with the minikube\nconfiguration, a port-forward on the conventional port. The deployment page has one\ndeployment and an access matrix over how it is reached; the architecture page''s lane\nsections become \"A lane refuses by name\" and \"One base, the lane''s hooks\"; the\nconfiguration page loses the local block and the per-lane asymmetries of shared memory,\ntimeouts, resources and sizing; the developer guide''s first loop is workspace init, run,\nwait, download.\n\nAGENTS.md states the rule for one lane: every operation is on the interface, the lane\nanswers it or refuses it by name, and what is correct for any lane lives on the base\nwith the null lane as its second implementer.\n\n* Comments and docstrings describe one lane\n\nWhat the code says about itself still contrasted two lanes: what \"the local lane\" did with\na bind mount, a compose step, a shell script or the host''s own counters, and what \"both\nlanes\" shared. Each sentence now describes the code as it is: one lane, a base that is\ncorrect for any lane, and the development-machine tools (vast configuration generate,\nvast results postprocess) that run a container through docker where a pod is not\navailable. Prose only; no behaviour changes.\n\n* The OpenAPI snapshot follows the reworded field docs\n\nThe field comments on the interface models are the schema''s descriptions, so the\ncommitted snapshot and the generated UI types move with them.\n\n* Read a recorded image''s labels and build lock from its registry, never from docker\n\nThe probes that shelled out to docker -- an image''s labels, its build lock, its local id\nand revision -- run in the service and the controller, whose images have no docker CLI,\nso they could only answer \"cannot tell\". They are removed.\n\nThe retrigger pre-flight asks the service for them instead: two hooks on ServiceBase,\nanswered by the cluster service through the registry with the deployment''s pull\ncredential, the reader a launch already uses. The host axis reads the protocol label from\nthere and reports an image the registry would not answer for as unknown, with that\nreason. The controller reads a build lock through its backend alone, and the controller''s\nbranches for a backend with no cluster configuration, which no backend is, are removed.\n\n* Refuse execution.local and unknown launch-request fields rather than ignoring them\n\nA .vast declaring execution.local validated and was silently dropped, since the execution\nschema ignores undeclared keys; the overrides it holds apply to no run. It is now refused\nwhen authoring and dropped with a warning when an archived campaign is read, as the other\narchived-only keys are. The config version stays at 4.\n\nCreateCampaignRequest and ExecRequest refuse a field they do not declare, so a request\ncarrying show_gui or a backend selector is answered with the field''s name instead of being\naccepted and ignored. The container CLI no longer sends backend. The OpenAPI snapshot and\nthe web UI''s generated types follow.\n\n* Name the service''s parts for what they are rather than for a lane\n\nWith one execution runtime there is no lane to name. The identifiers that carried the word\nsay what they are instead: UnsupportedOperation (wire code unsupported_operation) names the\nimplementation that declines, each implementation''s IMPLEMENTATION attribute says which it\nis, the test double is NullService, and the held-container runner is ExecRunner /\nKubeExecRunner. The MCP validation tools report where they checked as \"source\", and a\ncampaign record carries its execution_type under that name.\n\nThe serve command takes no --backend. It serves the one implementation installed through\nthe robovast.execution_backends entry point, and refuses to start with none installed,\nnaming robovast-cluster, or with several.\n\n* Describe one runtime in comments, docs and tests\n\nComments, docstrings, docs, CLI help, MCP tool descriptions and test names describe the\nservice as it runs, in a cluster, instead of contrasting it with a second runtime. Sentences\nthat only drew that contrast are gone, and the architecture pages describe the design as it\nis: ServiceBase with ClusterService as its implementation and NullService for tests, an\noperation an implementation does not offer refused with UnsupportedOperation, and the\nimplementation resolved through its entry point.\n\nTests named after the contrast are named after what they hold. The web UI''s generated API\ntypes follow the reworded docstrings.\n\n* The end-to-end retrigger check reads the recorded image from its registry\n\nThe pre-flight takes the readers for an image''s labels and build lock, so the CI script\nhands it the registry client''s, which answer for the public images the run pushed.\n\n* Remove what only a service outside the cluster could reach\n\nThe serve command runs only inside a cluster, so the code that answered for a service on a\nworkstation had no caller left:\n\n- Pinned workspaces: the serve command''s --workspace-dir, and the workspace registry''s\n  read-only in-place entry with the refusals that guarded it.\n- VersionInfo.results_root and sources_root, always null, with the route''s redaction of them.\n- describe_world''s backend argument and ExecRunner.exec_in''s env argument, both accepted and\n  ignored.\n- The on-disk campaign tarball writer, the text renderer of the code-provenance record, a\n  scheduling hint nothing raised, a duplicated helper, and the usage sampler''s priming read.\n\nScene generation and screenshots always run through the implementation''s runner, which\n_scene_runner_context now must provide. Building a workspace''s scene passes it too, instead\nof falling back to a docker run the service cannot perform.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtQY6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAXAIQAIIymvmvYEm+LhUCY3UBp031\nzz7d4bEQOvxl8IGn3y8opEuD0YorMkhbI/kUw1vkxK85oXwb/Ar5/B8sn5NZ3vXX\nF0xjmFjjKNvv8QlzRlVDHrJvrYATbN4OSPqCKfXaWQhSE+84wM4k+Qpmns4IrQ2g\nmcqKnQiE+FXPm2d1itcciFPmfZKAfM6C2S5TZBg6rxz1CX2sIZ1XEVGNPpaQXOD/\nsgaJbFl2hSDrCPUCarXoRJDBwZBUn6JO4GznB+u9sG1+pR5XUGwdcxoAhYPXYtRL\nEVkXs68F3N1YtvbxWVSTr5pJNYzEyKiA3R4p87vfyUOEiz+zvhoSr8YZiOJCR91B\nt9U1cxpBIrdercvkvZ4JFEakHf5wNlfZn79VW6CyMyfcdZc2FPP9yHId18KJ0Efi\nZPfvNeahI9LjjkZNaXq7BmJ24PDbmfYhC5o/XhOJmMTUvA2CsW6w4MmiopPMV1ox\nVlhuAt4TiWcvk6BLIhvA784dsUq0kFny77lYuH/+SdfNbpPMA6grcbKbupHLGxC2\nJURZL85Lgrt+wr0YmCrmSPd3TcUD+JjVyUjP7d6BnGsTGKQyl3RlkD8O6mTSRq+C\n5eb+E8zmXCK7pTUiiE/olp9N9NKnEv0sT+EwnrOm0S4OtoT5Td8tzosvjBwp+y6h\nt6zXmeoxgo1E2Web1pWj\n=oR8t\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T11:15:07Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/ee485e51dea361172d5450ef3f265afe6d106673","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGVlNDg1ZTUxZGVhMzYxMTcyZDU0NTBlZjNmMjY1YWZlNmQxMDY2NzM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3","sha":"a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3"}],"sha":"ee485e51dea361172d5450ef3f265afe6d106673","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/ee485e51dea361172d5450ef3f265afe6d106673"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T10:31:51Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T10:31:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make the docs pass the spelling check (#686)\n\n`make checkspelling` failed on valid terms the word list lacked and on a\nfew British spellings in docs that otherwise spell the US way. Use the\nUS forms (memoize, summarize, traveled), spell out \"release candidate\",\nand add the remaining terms to the word list. The capitalized\n\"Subclasses\" entry becomes lowercase, which accepts both forms.","tree":{"sha":"8c23809a28a155a9d514ef255f4f525d0547d705","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/8c23809a28a155a9d514ef255f4f525d0547d705"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/a88fdecb745ffcca2c9646e7f105b41afe546af3","verification":{"payload":"tree 8c23809a28a155a9d514ef255f4f525d0547d705\nparent 5c0852fcfa01c0626a49f406708fd813207195bc\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790245911 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790245911 +0200\n\nMake the docs pass the spelling check (#686)\n\n`make checkspelling` failed on valid terms the word list lacked and on a\nfew British spellings in docs that otherwise spell the US way. Use the\nUS forms (memoize, summarize, traveled), spell out \"release candidate\",\nand add the remaining terms to the word list. The capitalized\n\"Subclasses\" entry becomes lowercase, which accepts both forms.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtPwXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlL0QAGlC+x7Hw/DpnkW+Y6V3cFsc\naRwbfk0NEMKcbOUUuutJcGrMR7WAYtTnn2xfY1+COy4k5bEmbo3KtAtx3GtEB0AP\nGkqkTxsgpdTcgGP5RHiiWaJCE2ULL4iMpjZ0q+91ok9R+ySTn8a/Xx/hiH3BIO5m\naFBSw8eWlUtpQAVMAvngtVo5lEhnv/9GQb9HVVf+H0peSA