users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/051c4ab1f01777744d0e4e7b9c71677d4c11adbc","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDA1MWM0YWIxZjAxNzc3NzQ0ZDBlNGU3YjljNzE2NzdkNGMxMWFkYmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e","sha":"e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e"}],"sha":"051c4ab1f01777744d0e4e7b9c71677d4c11adbc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/051c4ab1f01777744d0e4e7b9c71677d4c11adbc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T08:52:02Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T08:52:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"The end-to-end test asserts that postprocessing ran, and the cluster lane lets it (#682)\n\n* Give a postprocessing pod that cannot start the run loop''s patience before calling it failed\n\nThe wait on a postprocessing Job read the pod''s first Unschedulable condition as a verdict\nand reported the campaign''s postprocessing as failed without it ever having run. On a\none-node cluster that is the ordinary case: the queue reserved the pod''s room, but\nKubernetes charges a deleted pod''s requests to its node until the pod is gone, and the\ncampaign''s last run pods are still terminating when the postprocessing pod is created, so\nthe scheduler''s first pass finds the node full and its next pass places it.\n\nThe wait now tolerates a blocked pod the way the batch loop and the image build already\ndo, with their two tolerances: a pod waiting its turn for a busy node or a throttled pull\ngets the long grace, anything else the short one, and a pod that starts resets the clock.\nThe verdict names how long the pod was given.\n\n* Carry the admission headroom from the operator''s environment into the service Deployment\n\nThe docs said the per-node headroom is set on the service Deployment, and nothing put it\nthere: the service read ROBOVAST_NODE_HEADROOM_CPU and _MEMORY from its own environment,\nwhich setup rendered without them, so the default held on every deployment. On a single\nnode whose control plane already sits in the requests admission subtracts, the default\nreserves the same core twice, and a postprocessing pod the node can place waits in the\nqueue for good.\n\nSetup and upgrade now write both variables from the operator''s environment, like the\nbootstrap figures, stated even when empty and checked at deploy so an unparseable value\nrefuses the deploy rather than every admission after it. Empty reads as the default; zero\nis a value.\n\n* Size the growth_sim example''s postprocessing for what it does\n\nThe example converts no rosbags, so its postprocessing takes the smallest figure the pod\naccepts rather than the shared default sized for conversion, and fits a four-core node.\n\n* The end-to-end test asserts that postprocessing ran, and its cluster leaves room for it\n\nvast campaign wait exits 0 for a campaign whose runs finished and whose postprocessing\nthen failed, so the exit code the script branched on said nothing about the one step it\nexists to exercise: a run whose postprocessing pod never started reported green. The\nscript now asks the campaign once more after each wait and fails on the postprocessing\nverdict, and the structure check requires the marker the example''s postprocessing step\nwrites.\n\nThe one node of the CI cluster carries the control plane, the deployment''s own pods and\nthe campaign; the postprocessing pod reserves two cores, request equal to limit. The build\ndaemon''s ceiling is trimmed, since the job never builds, and admission''s headroom is set\nto nothing, since the tenants it protects are the control plane and already sit in the\nrequests.","tree":{"sha":"e9c0203175ef63ad57f4ff7a10472a7d030153b2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/e9c0203175ef63ad57f4ff7a10472a7d030153b2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e","verification":{"payload":"tree e9c0203175ef63ad57f4ff7a10472a7d030153b2\nparent 7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790239922 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790239922 +0200\n\nThe end-to-end test asserts that postprocessing ran, and the cluster lane lets it (#682)\n\n* Give a postprocessing pod that cannot start the run loop''s patience before calling it failed\n\nThe wait on a postprocessing Job read the pod''s first Unschedulable condition as a verdict\nand reported the campaign''s postprocessing as failed without it ever having run. On a\none-node cluster that is the ordinary case: the queue reserved the pod''s room, but\nKubernetes charges a deleted pod''s requests to its node until the pod is gone, and the\ncampaign''s last run pods are still terminating when the postprocessing pod is created, so\nthe scheduler''s first pass finds the node full and its next pass places it.\n\nThe wait now tolerates a blocked pod the way the batch loop and the image build already\ndo, with their two tolerances: a pod waiting its turn for a busy node or a throttled pull\ngets the long grace, anything else the short one, and a pod that starts resets the clock.\nThe verdict names how long the pod was given.\n\n* Carry the admission headroom from the operator''s environment into the service Deployment\n\nThe docs said the per-node headroom is set on the service Deployment, and nothing put it\nthere: the service read ROBOVAST_NODE_HEADROOM_CPU and _MEMORY from its own environment,\nwhich setup rendered without them, so the default held on every deployment. On a single\nnode whose control plane already sits in the requests admission subtracts, the default\nreserves the same core twice, and a postprocessing pod the node can place waits in the\nqueue for good.\n\nSetup and upgrade now write both variables from the operator''s environment, like the\nbootstrap figures, stated even when empty and checked at deploy so an unparseable value\nrefuses the deploy rather than every admission after it. Empty reads as the default; zero\nis a value.\n\n* Size the growth_sim example''s postprocessing for what it does\n\nThe example converts no rosbags, so its postprocessing takes the smallest figure the pod\naccepts rather than the shared default sized for conversion, and fits a four-core node.\n\n* The end-to-end test asserts that postprocessing ran, and its cluster leaves room for it\n\nvast campaign wait exits 0 for a campaign whose runs finished and whose postprocessing\nthen failed, so the exit code the script branched on said nothing about the one step it\nexists to exercise: a run whose postprocessing pod never started reported green. The\nscript now asks the campaign once more after each wait and fails on the postprocessing\nverdict, and the structure check requires the marker the example''s postprocessing step\nwrites.\n\nThe one node of the CI cluster carries the control plane, the deployment''s own pods and\nthe campaign; the postprocessing pod reserves two cores, request equal to limit. The build\ndaemon''s ceiling is trimmed, since the job never builds, and admission''s headroom is set\nto nothing, since the tenants it protects are the control plane and already sit in the\nrequests.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtOSyCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyCMQAImZdCbNZwAK6U5oQpV7z2A/\nTu6JfJN9Jul6RzKZKHhhIh7zhOT4njqAErCf2L9rFY8yBv/KGCZTA7JcO8br+tcY\nEsLaRiRzifLTWZUHcbxI4aT0Nmo134bjjIsaq4jW6KsakrsM/F1KC3Bje2LAta3n\nCsf/cLGH6dPgdC243/fPHia4JyJRFc6qcl82XWAfb72Lp9T3sLoi2jQHkCRuyEuq\nQY2/bs/PHbNpUU2tORqahk1Wpt0mH35uTtfnttZ2PMxPPmv/oqzRUlOLWRftlfPL\nk4bbo2iTG3F8dmcn9MHPBxJZNsr8ks8i9VtDasVpInSspcDzQ9n8HofynIojfZa0\nXQsnWhBzhnVeLMFaX4blbNb30TlMtBNCHg+rKnqfCKrT6zzGO+RVgPDcHqUIDjCt\nHEdneIJ+okDJbU/ypg3bO6XQNf+tzMOTZIdA3n9kquAyj39dBK+IKygZf+DMxb7Q\nJyU5C9McQK+O/AITgy+klmVaOmjyra3RssITkMkALZZzc3Iqr0JpGCVG5enMa1DA\nteILvgBgfaQ1EO1J3/hb9qebbeMyS/T1K+bMgLN/Vyo/BNywwcDdcXeL90Ccherq\nCT0GMCk/8q8GgGyJiCcPcNb4SG12QTNerIpiuItQ0DumwpwbNDjeTFieeCrMtgKu\nssVvQ0BxIO3pn3rlOqp6\n=4LcM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T08:52:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGUxZDUzYmNlMzM3NTVlYzRjOGY4ZWZlOGJmOTUyOWYwYzgzN2VmNmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812","sha":"7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812"}],"sha":"e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/e1d53bce33755ec4c8f8efe8bf9529f0c837ef6e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T08:11:31Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T08:11:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Run the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward (#672)\n\n* Run the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward\n\nThe one end-to-end test of the whole stack ran its campaign through a local Docker\nservice started by the script. It now deploys the service into a kind cluster on the\nrunner -- one node, hostPath directories, the same shape as a developer''s minikube -- and\ndrives the same five steps through a port-forward on the conventional port. The cluster\nlane runs only inside a cluster, since a campaign''s pods deliver their outputs to the\nservice over the cluster network, so the service is deployed in rather than pointed at.\n\nWhat the cluster changes about the test, and how each is met: the deployment''s data root\nis a directory on the node, so a runner directory is mounted there and the results are\nread from the host as before; one deployment serves every workflow of a run, so a\nworkflow now returns its campaign''s directory and the packing comparison is handed the\ntwo it should compare rather than finding \"the\" campaign under a root; the cluster lane\nnamespaces a job''s parameter file by batch, so the comparison skips that file by its\nshape; the kubelet has no registry login, so setup is given the runner''s and turns it into\nthe pull secret every pod uses; the deployment mints its own access token, which is read\nback with `vast service token` and never echoed, since a login prints it in the curl line\nit suggests; and the results arrive owned by the pods'' user, so the retrigger step takes\nownership before staging beside them.\n\nThe job now needs the sidecar and controller images of this run as well as the base,\nbecause the service is one of them and its pods transfer with the other.\n\n* Install kind with the runner''s privileges: its bin directory is not the runner''s to chmod\n\n* The end-to-end job installs the cluster lane, which is its own distribution","tree":{"sha":"93a23536117c1f31394a87638492dbd058c41bfc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/93a23536117c1f31394a87638492dbd058c41bfc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812","verification":{"payload":"tree 93a23536117c1f31394a87638492dbd058c41bfc\nparent 3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790237491 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790237491 +0200\n\nRun the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward (#672)\n\n* Run the end-to-end test on a cluster: the service deployed into kind, reached by port-forward\n\nThe one end-to-end test of the whole stack ran its campaign through a local Docker\nservice started by the script. It now deploys the service into a kind cluster on the\nrunner -- one node, hostPath directories, the same shape as a developer''s minikube -- and\ndrives the same five steps through a port-forward on the conventional port. The cluster\nlane runs only inside a cluster, since a campaign''s pods deliver their outputs to the\nservice over the cluster network, so the service is deployed in rather than pointed at.\n\nWhat the cluster changes about the test, and how each is met: the deployment''s data root\nis a directory on the node, so a runner directory is mounted there and the results are\nread from the host as before; one deployment serves every workflow of a run, so a\nworkflow now returns its campaign''s directory and the packing comparison is handed the\ntwo it should compare rather than finding \"the\" campaign under a root; the cluster lane\nnamespaces a job''s parameter file by batch, so the comparison skips that file by its\nshape; the kubelet has no registry login, so setup is given the runner''s and turns it into\nthe pull secret every pod uses; the deployment mints its own access token, which is read\nback with `vast service token` and never echoed, since a login prints it in the curl line\nit suggests; and the results arrive owned by the pods'' user, so the retrigger step takes\nownership before staging beside them.\n\nThe job now needs the sidecar and controller images of this run as well as the base,\nbecause the service is one of them and its pods transfer with the other.\n\n* Install kind with the runner''s privileges: its bin directory is not the runner''s to chmod\n\n* The end-to-end job installs the cluster lane, which is its own distribution","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtNszCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAddIQAI7xxJPMwn1ShklFcroUYEpF\nBMkNsi8J7n5fRorce7mF/k1ykzUXt8AHD509kHvjsbGMW5I004HaVnklJInn49FA\nuzqb29Uwt+9PTANLf4ku8M/kr/bwOELdMLVGYWvnec2NQhRR1T6wE4/lCprCPVLM\nFppDqYg0vlZB40Ulg0gs/jZfb24UhOe1t18ByhtnXWNSuNmW6UnX5zhHJzH1p1Hz\nVnTA72+KcBT0ofjRXS4WsGMFRqllk9W1v5V0qm1LBSjeuDIMEdn505RL7nZ9O24x\nljsqPaprqp/4EZQ8WW07jSBwpIqlgNy1fjBM/U7CQdTlKmdEW0mmadO7JakOWrqx\nYcNN9gr0MGhgn3nJJU8tRAZSJFSoYHVjlc4kG2xxqFIEVEJ9/O7ko30JYh75lJrr\n6SI627FJwViHECnJybd4HJgU0/E63WdDeR/JGiDpxshlpqx5WQX4fGJi5ZQ94rVq\nb1c6MtmGg2zhBjUoykCuB4nmjbI86i13yESAbxPZjeUIb+To9krVePNzH72lss5H\nC0pQtlgZYFs0P08nRgJ9XniR4H8ti1OTA0xVPX1vo5yPRETx47zK0o4TN6JTjuGw\nV11bwgQSlY6UPrD+j+nu6pzG31eFKwBQIqaD1mZKx12/c2dSChv8L6pP3GxGTA97\nqIORnGllxVNR2z3rsOMl\n=IFtU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T08:11:31Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDdkODQ4YzkyNDhlYWQzOTQ5MWM0NWViODc4NTBiMjNmZmY5NjE4MTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba","sha":"3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba"}],"sha":"7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/7d848c9248ead39491c45eb87850b23fff961812"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T07:59:34Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T07:59:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Read the simulator''s documentation out of the image that runs it (#521)\n\n* Serve the documentation of the packages a campaign is authored against\n\nA campaign is written against more than RoboVAST -- the simulator''s world format, its\nplugin reference, the scenario DSL -- and each of those is documented in the repository\nthat owns it. The corpus held this repository''s pages only, so a search for a world''s\n`components:` list returned nothing, and nothing in the reply distinguished that from\nthere being no such thing.\n\nA package now publishes its own pages by registering a `robovast.docs` entry point\nresolving to an object with a `DOCS_DIR`. The direction is what matters: a package says it\nwants its documentation served, rather than this one naming a repository it must not\ndepend on. The entry-point name prefixes its pages, so two repositories that both have an\n`architecture` page keep both -- letting one win would answer a question about the\nsubstrate with this repository''s page.\n\n`ROBOVAST_DOCS_EXTRA` takes `label=/path` pairs for a deployment whose packages predate\nthe entry point: configuration, which is where the wiring belongs when it cannot be a\npublished name.\n\nOnly our own pages have their directives expanded; another repository''s Sphinx extensions\nare its own, and a half-rendered page is worse than the source. A broken entry point costs\nthat package its docs and no one else theirs.\n\n* Update mcp.rst\n\n* Write the section note as a comment, which is what it is\n\nSuper-linter runs pylint over the tests, and a bare string at module level is a\nstatement with no effect (W0105) rather than a docstring -- only the first one in a\nmodule is that. The text is unchanged.\n\n* Say how the search is usable, since the query is what decides that\n\nThe search is a substring match over lines, grouped by page in NAME order -- so the first\npage back is not the best one, and the query carries the whole weight. Measured over the\ncorpus: `world` matches 31 of 50 pages and `osc` 25, while `spawn_robot` matches 8 and\n`sensor_coverage_probe` 2. A reader who does not know that asks for a word, gets half the\ncorpus, and concludes the tool is no use.\n\nSo the page says it, with the measurements: search an identifier as a file spells it. That\nis also what an author is looking for anyway -- a plugin name, a key with its colon, a\nheader. Plus the three calls in the order they are usually wanted, and the two things that\nsurprise: `limit` caps excerpts PER PAGE rather than the number of pages, so raising it on a\nbroad query buys a bigger reply and not a narrower one; and `source` says which corpus a\npage came from, which is how to tell two same-named pages apart.\n\nThe tool description keeps only what changes whether a search happens at all -- that the\ncorpus now reaches past this repository. An agent that does not know the substrate''s pages\nare there will not search for them; one that searches badly still gets something back, and\nthe page is there to read.\n\n* Take the substrate''s documentation from the commit this repository already pins\n\n`search_docs` served RoboVAST''s pages alone, so a search for a world''s `components:` list or\nthe scenario DSL returned nothing -- which reads as \"no such thing\" rather than \"not indexed\nhere\", and leaves an agent on the MCP path unable to discover the format it is asked to\nwrite.\n\nThe pages arrive with the image. This repository pins the simulator''s commit for the\nsimulator image and clones it there, so the service image clones the same commit''s `docs/`\n-- sparse and blobless, docs only -- into /opt/robovast/substrate-docs, one directory per\ncorpus with a `.ref` beside the pages. `tools/refresh_source_pins.py` already finds any\n`*_REF` in any container Dockerfile and resolves each repo once per run, so the two pins\ncannot drift by editing one.\n\nNot an entry point: the service image installs `robovast-sim-roqsim`, the backend adapter,\nand deliberately not roqsim -- no MuJoCo runtime enters it -- so nothing in a deployed\nservice could have loaded one. Not a path into a sibling checkout either; that is what a\ncomponent which must stay publishable on its own may not name at run time. The build is\nwhere this repository already names the ref, because it must.\n\nA corpus''s directory name prefixes its pages, so both repositories keep an `architecture`\npage and neither shadows the other. Every listing row says which corpus an answer came from\nand, where the build recorded one, the commit it was taken at: a release that builds the\nsimulator from a moving branch can ship an image newer than these pages, and the ref is what\nmakes that visible.\n\nOnly RoboVAST''s own pages have their directives expanded; another repository''s Sphinx\nextensions are its own, and half-rendering them is worse than leaving them written.\n\n* Refuse two commits of one source, and call these repositories upstream\n\nA source is pinned in more than one Dockerfile because each must build on its own and\nDockerfile.roqsim is built with an empty context, so there is no shared file to read a pin\nfrom. What matters is not that the pin sits in one place but that the copies cannot\ndisagree: two that do bake two commits of one source into one image family.\n`refresh_source_pins.py --check` reads the committed pins, asks no remote, and fails when\none repo carries two shas. `make check-source-pins`, and CI runs it.\n\n`--write` could never produce that -- it resolves each repo once per run -- but an edit by\nhand can, which is what the check is for.\n\n\"Substrate\" becomes \"upstream\" throughout: it is the word this repository already uses for\nthese commits (\"the upstream commits baked in\"), and one term for one thing beats two.\n\n* Set a source pin in one command, since it lives in more than one file\n\nChanging ROQSIM_REF by hand now means changing every Dockerfile that carries it and\nremembering which those are -- a worse job than before, and the check added beside it turns\nforgetting into a CI failure rather than a quiet divergence.\n\n`--set NAME_REF=<sha|branch|tag>` writes that pin wherever it appears and leaves every other\npin alone. A sha is taken as given; a branch or tag is resolved once against the repo the\npin names, so the same commit lands in every copy. `make source-pin PIN=ROQSIM_REF=main`.\n\nThe copies exist because each Dockerfile has to build on its own and Dockerfile.roqsim is\nbuilt with an empty context. That is the build''s business; it should not be the editor''s.\n\n* Read the simulator''s documentation out of the image that runs it\n\n`search_docs` served RoboVAST''s pages alone, so a search for a world''s `components:` list or\nthe scenario DSL returned nothing -- which reads as \"no such thing\" rather than \"not indexed\nhere\". With `address=/sources/<workspace_id>/<path>` it also serves the pages of the image\nthat `.vast` runs, under a `roqsim-` prefix.\n\nThe image already carries them: the simulator image copies roqsim''s source tree to\n/opt/roqsim, so its `docs/` is there to be read. `docs` joins the catalog commands, so the\nexec, the held container and the per-image cache are the ones the catalogs already use, and\nthe answer comes from the simulator THAT campaign pins rather than whichever version sits\nbeside the service. Nothing is added to an image and no commit is pinned twice.\n\nWithout an address nothing is fetched and the search costs what it did. A miss then names\nthe argument that reaches the rest, so zero results stop reading as \"there is no such thing\".\n\n`docs` is not in the tool''s catalog vocabulary: it is a command this reads, not a listing\nanyone asks for.\n\n* Name the address when a page of the image is read without it\n\nA search with an address returns names like `roqsim-interfaces`; reading one without the\naddress listed RoboVAST''s own pages and called it unknown, which is the answer this whole\nchange exists to remove.\n\n* Rank the corpus, and name the pages when excerpts would be a sample\n\nA search matched by substring and returned pages in name order, so the first result was\nthe first alphabetically and a common term answered with five excerpts from each of\ntwenty-seven pages -- about 15,000 tokens of arbitrary sample, with a note at the end\nsaying it was one.\n\nFTS5 with bm25() picks the pages and orders them; sqlite ships it, so this costs no\ndependency. A query becomes quoted prefix terms, ANDed, because a word may carry\npunctuation an operator would claim and a caller was never offered a query language.\n\nAbove a measured reply budget the excerpts go and the pages are named instead, best first,\nwith the call that reads one. `campaign` goes from ~15,700 tokens to ~297; `components`, at\nnine pages, still returns its excerpts.","tree":{"sha":"bfd058c2259645bea51ea4ec820cad589e82d7b6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/bfd058c2259645bea51ea4ec820cad589e82d7b6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/3fab72ca798c0b56cf270870bcbbddfeeb254aba","verification":{"payload":"tree bfd058c2259645bea51ea4ec820cad589e82d7b6\nparent ea7b7678a8722ad41ab4c5fa87dea95118f7e55e\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790236774 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790236774 +0200\n\nRe