commit/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0","sha":"d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0"}],"sha":"ea7b7678a8722ad41ab4c5fa87dea95118f7e55e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/ea7b7678a8722ad41ab4c5fa87dea95118f7e55e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T06:12:23Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T06:12:23Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Stop serving a Returns: section as prose, and merge one question''s four tools (#446)\n\n`Args:` is what anchors the section split, so a `Returns:` with none before it is never\nclaimed and is served whole, in the description every caller pays for on every request.\nFive tools were doing it -- three that take no arguments and two that document none --\nand the guard could not see them: it matched the marker indented by four spaces, which is\nwhere it sits only in the one case the guard was written for. It matches at any\nindentation now.\n\nWhat those sections said is kept where it changed a caller''s decision: can_build_images\nbefore authoring a container that adds packages, in_pod: false meaning campaigns are\ndriven from outside the cluster, reserved-versus-measured for sizing a sweep, null meaning\n\"no reading\" and never zero. The field-by-field enumeration around them is gone -- FastMCP\ndrops `Returns:` for every other tool, so no tool tells a model its response shape and\nthese five were the anomaly.\n\nSeparately, the image catalog''s four tools were two helpers called with a different\nconstant, so the surface carried four descriptions of one question -- and the two naming\ntheir catalog in the tool name were the two callers most often got wrong, because nothing\nabout the name said an `address` was still required. They are one pair over a `catalog`\nargument, which also gives an unknown catalog a refusal naming the ones that exist.\n\nThe budget goes down to match, as a ratchet rather than headroom: it is spent by every\ncaller on every request before a single tool is chosen, so down is the only direction that\nhelps.","tree":{"sha":"2919227c60d7701959658780083fae221354a0fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/2919227c60d7701959658780083fae221354a0fa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0","verification":{"payload":"tree 2919227c60d7701959658780083fae221354a0fa\nparent 888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790230343 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790230343 +0200\n\nStop serving a Returns: section as prose, and merge one question''s four tools (#446)\n\n`Args:` is what anchors the section split, so a `Returns:` with none before it is never\nclaimed and is served whole, in the description every caller pays for on every request.\nFive tools were doing it -- three that take no arguments and two that document none --\nand the guard could not see them: it matched the marker indented by four spaces, which is\nwhere it sits only in the one case the guard was written for. It matches at any\nindentation now.\n\nWhat those sections said is kept where it changed a caller''s decision: can_build_images\nbefore authoring a container that adds packages, in_pod: false meaning campaigns are\ndriven from outside the cluster, reserved-versus-measured for sizing a sweep, null meaning\n\"no reading\" and never zero. The field-by-field enumeration around them is gone -- FastMCP\ndrops `Returns:` for every other tool, so no tool tells a model its response shape and\nthese five were the anomaly.\n\nSeparately, the image catalog''s four tools were two helpers called with a different\nconstant, so the surface carried four descriptions of one question -- and the two naming\ntheir catalog in the tool name were the two callers most often got wrong, because nothing\nabout the name said an `address` was still required. They are one pair over a `catalog`\nargument, which also gives an unknown catalog a refusal naming the ones that exist.\n\nThe budget goes down to match, as a ratchet rather than headroom: it is spent by every\ncaller on every request before a single tool is chosen, so down is the only direction that\nhelps.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtL9HCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/5MQAJdlQ7IgfvKxG/ShMFEfoAXS\nlwGOAa+axycT1x6OVl+EhlAUjgLPFS8XA6OaO6HyzaqiNUOCpklBzz8w+9YW4HWc\n5+howW8XVl0G9mIsGa0MZFspAE82GfzxfbiFzrjyANLw4bkrUG0QkfK5eCHw3xTH\n0eA0861HlgFYiZiwfjVZNx+AY8TXfGu6XlaOQQ8w2sDs+8b0pByC5iIlQX8b5CxX\naoD0ZukmOyC4SRJWgeta+1HsyI2PtbHBYKNDj32r/nBZTa/T6b/vI1L6f6qDISq8\nkc/YE5oQY/0qXlRXDLt3w0mDW3DIdda4527penYM8v+K8IbIeuH9zZJyQFefqRBL\n4Wp+1zreQnJ0aOclASCQ8/Yj2CARbpIkb1S84JXgOWDrKsWnDmn0dmuH4HtxQeH2\nDM2k3n2+sZRIF9f9etXf25P2Gd7fRsomyJFc+E/2snrycD51ToCLgaUzuyFQIVJb\n7eeArbrQPehCekZeh+5HcUWoDQ8tEnTHq232495KXH8JDfo1G/BBehTZzJUpejPm\n8R2zT/lR6cd6H47YKxD/IhfdnLu1VqHCLHpEYPLSk70IUImn0k4qRtbtU3TPsSNk\n02OrRwTnlSwfxd8N2zJJbxq7PJxsPs/qRdAEa1zA1y2LSAvFHkLbtvQEQITsjD86\nJpNNCTAx5nqRl+X5lfld\n=Zu5X\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T06:12:24Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGQyY2I5NWNmMzc1OTdjZDY0YzdhNzQ2MjI1MDMyMDg0YTVjMjlkYTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00","sha":"888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00"}],"sha":"d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/d2cb95cf37597cd64c7a746225032084a5c29da0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T06:02:38Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T06:02:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give every node an identity, so two unlabelled ones are two nodes (#257)\n\n* Give every node an identity, so two unlabelled ones are two nodes\n\n`NodeBudget.node_id` is what the admission controller keys its per-node\naccounting on -- `_effective_free_locked` and `drain` both build dicts from\nit -- and it was `None` for every node the last `vast cluster setup` had not\nlabelled. Two such nodes therefore collided in one slot: whichever came last\nin the reading won, and the rest of their free capacity was never offered to\nanybody. A four-node cluster that had not been re-set up since the identity\nlabel was introduced ran on one node''s worth of room, with nothing in any log\nsaying so, and the bigger the cluster the more of it disappeared.\n\n`None` was doing two jobs at once: \"which node is this\" and \"no node at all\".\nSo split them.\n\n* Every node now has an identity. Where the label is on the node it is read\n  off it; where it is not, `node_label(name)` computes the same value -- an\n  unsalted digest of the name, which is exactly what makes it recomputable.\n  That also makes the identity stable across the `setup` that eventually\n  stamps it, so a reservation held while the labelling happens keeps its key.\n* `NodeBudget.pinnable` carries the half that genuinely depends on the label:\n  whether a `nodeSelector` can name the node. An unpinnable node still holds\n  work; the job is simply created unpinned and kube-scheduler settles it.\n\nTwo facts that used to ride on `node_id is None` now say what they mean. A\ngrant on an unpinnable node is charged to that node, so the rest of the pass\ncannot hand the same cores out again; and it is no longer counted against\nGROWTH_UNPINNED_LIMIT, which bounds work in flight towards a node the\nautoscaler has yet to produce -- an unlabelled cluster with an autoscaler\nstopped admitting after sixteen jobs because of it.\n\n`_effective_free_locked` also warns when a budget reports two nodes under one\nid, rather than absorbing it: the whole cost of this bug was that it was\nsilent.\n\n* Stamp the node identity label on upgrade too, not only on setup\n\n`robovast.io/node-id` was applied by `vast cluster setup` and nowhere else, so a\ncluster moved forward with `vast service upgrade` -- the command for rolling out\na version -- kept whatever labelling it happened to have. That is not a corner:\nthe docs already record it as routine on a managed node pool, where autoscaling,\nauto-upgrade, auto-repair and spot reclaim replace nodes constantly and every\nreplacement arrives unlabelled.\n\nAn operator should not have to know the label exists. Reconcile it beside the\nRBAC, which is there for the same reason -- a version needing cluster state the\nlast one did not would otherwise deploy and misbehave at runtime. That also puts\nit above the `--no-restart` line, correctly: a node label is the node''s own\nstate, so the running pod picks it up with no roll, and an operator missing it\nneed not roll a service to get it.\n\nIdempotent by value, so an already-labelled cluster patches nothing. It is\ndeliberately not a placement decision: `resolve_placement` is what an upgrade\nmust not do, because it would move the data it was asked to leave alone --\nstamping identities moves nothing and chooses nothing.\n\nEvery existing test that drives `upgrade` now stubs the call; unstubbed it dials\na real API server, which is what the other cluster-touching steps there already\nguard against.\n\n* Carry the pin''s gate through the node the item is offered\n\n`may_use` takes the node rather than its id, because a pin and a gate ask different things\nof it: a pin is a `nodeSelector` value and can only be honoured where the label is, while a\ngate waits for a measurement that an unpinnable node can never have. They are still ANDed.\nA pinned item that matched its node and skipped the gate would put a confined, calibrated\ncampaign on its node before the probe measured it, which is what `accepts_node` exists to\nprevent.\n\nAn unlabelled node is charged for the grant and created unpinned: there is no label to\nselect, so kube-scheduler settles the pod, and the charge is what stops the same cores\nbeing handed out twice in one pass.\n\nUpgrade reconciles the identity labels, so every test that stubs an upgrade''s steps one by\none stubs that call too -- unstubbed it reaches a real API server, which the suite''s own\nguard refuses.\n\n* Type a node''s identity as always present\n\nEvery node gets one -- read off the label or computed from the name -- so `str | None`\nstill admitted the value this all exists to remove. \"No node at all\" is a different\nquestion, asked where there genuinely is none (`chosen is None`).","tree":{"sha":"a429c8174ae0830f47ef89cb27daca2e5504174e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/a429c8174ae0830f47ef89cb27daca2e5504174e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00","verification":{"payload":"tree a429c8174ae0830f47ef89cb27daca2e5504174e\nparent aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790229758 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790229758 +0200\n\nGive every node an identity, so two unlabelled ones are two nodes (#257)\n\n* Give every node an identity, so two unlabelled ones are two nodes\n\n`NodeBudget.node_id` is what the admission controller keys its per-node\naccounting on -- `_effective_free_locked` and `drain` both build dicts from\nit -- and it was `None` for every node the last `vast cluster setup` had not\nlabelled. Two such nodes therefore collided in one slot: whichever came last\nin the reading won, and the rest of their free capacity was never offered to\nanybody. A four-node cluster that had not been re-set up since the identity\nlabel was introduced ran on one node''s worth of room, with nothing in any log\nsaying so, and the bigger the cluster the more of it disappeared.\n\n`None` was doing two jobs at once: \"which node is this\" and \"no node at all\".\nSo split them.\n\n* Every node now has an identity. Where the label is on the node it is read\n  off it; where it is not, `node_label(name)` computes the same value -- an\n  unsalted digest of the name, which is exactly what makes it recomputable.\n  That also makes the identity stable across the `setup` that eventually\n  stamps it, so a reservation held while the labelling happens keeps its key.\n* `NodeBudget.pinnable` carries the half that genuinely depends on the label:\n  whether a `nodeSelector` can name the node. An unpinnable node still holds\n  work; the job is simply created unpinned and kube-scheduler settles it.\n\nTwo facts that used to ride on `node_id is None` now say what they mean. A\ngrant on an unpinnable node is charged to that node, so the rest of the pass\ncannot hand the same cores out again; and it is no longer counted against\nGROWTH_UNPINNED_LIMIT, which bounds work in flight towards a node the\nautoscaler has yet to produce -- an unlabelled cluster with an autoscaler\nstopped admitting after sixteen jobs because of it.\n\n`_effective_free_locked` also warns when a budget reports two nodes under one\nid, rather than absorbing it: the whole cost of this bug was that it was\nsilent.\n\n* Stamp the node identity label on upgrade too, not only on setup\n\n`robovast.io/node-id` was applied by `vast cluster setup` and nowhere else, so a\ncluster moved forward with `vast service upgrade` -- the command for rolling out\na version -- kept whatever labelling it happened to have. That is not a corner:\nthe docs already record it as routine on a managed node pool, where autoscaling,\nauto-upgrade, auto-repair and spot reclaim replace nodes constantly and every\nreplacement arrives unlabelled.\n\nAn operator should not have to know the label exists. Reconcile it beside the\nRBAC, which is there for the same reason -- a version needing cluster state the\nlast one did not would otherwise deploy and misbehave at runtime. That also puts\nit above the `--no-restart` line, correctly: a node label is the node''s own\nstate, so the running pod picks it up with no roll, and an operator missing it\nneed not roll a service to get it.\n\nIdempotent by value, so an already-labelled cluster patches nothing. It is\ndeliberately not a placement decision: `resolve_placement` is what an upgrade\nmust not do, because it would move the data it was asked to leave alone --\nstamping identities moves nothing and chooses nothing.\n\nEvery existing test that drives `upgrade` now stubs the call; unstubbed it dials\na real API server, which is what the other cluster-touching steps there already\nguard against.\n\n* Carry the pin''s gate through the node the item is offered\n\n`may_use` takes the node rather than its id, because a pin and a gate ask different things\nof it: a pin is a `nodeSelector` value and can only be honoured where the label is, while a\ngate waits for a measurement that an unpinnable node can never have. They are still ANDed.\nA pinned item that matched its node and skipped the gate would put a confined, calibrated\ncampaign on its node before the probe measured it, which is what `accepts_node` exists to\nprevent.\n\nAn unlabelled node is charged for the grant and created unpinned: there is no label to\nselect, so kube-scheduler settles the pod, and the charge is what stops the same cores\nbeing handed out twice in one pass.\n\nUpgrade reconciles the identity labels, so every test that stubs an upgrade''s steps one by\none stubs that call too -- unstubbed it reaches a real API server, which the suite''s own\nguard refuses.\n\n* Type a node''s identity as always present\n\nEvery node gets one -- read off the label or computed from the name -- so `str | None`\nstill admitted the value this all exists to remove. \"No node at all\" is a different\nquestion, asked where there genuinely is none (`chosen is None`).","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtLz+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAABlkQABacqOMisteka6mtLscSd6ek\nsBT0WLLvktXITcWZ8KXqQC3sYNHWqrqRoTaChVSJ1m1NwuJuSw41NLnzSJHRTUcy\nuaUAxp3GWhiArJZGcLfQ7UMgdrivMR/13y6Cq2RflWzLi2xAtRzo7DfCSwH4jUd8\nnKd1bee0dioyMgGtVE/vC8E2XE+F+oiV/68lRCGAAb6xhlfLezLh1zAbWsYQtMIP\nTj25wIHba8e0Pdi9ufq56OwteFr20UPpTSVTgCZy3gunymsNryAF/rUiiOt33RXR\nAhBla6Cx1O03d/IrqBhkUlijSr4WTixybF1Hi1IvO5RC9UFwclCRjOai+iP+2kPn\nNLBz8R1N80XCzxZteSJ7BsW4zpN7rklFmEqVC9OeLPXf2gLQXriPtY64hFzR35U5\n0cQ9g1NpXfyJLVTvlm3PAQulPTCzC5AttmYb19S52SVz++82UEkde2ocl8+Hf+xj\niPVZHJKU+TG1G0j8/+qjdCt1hzjB2120y0R7PBhUCkP5X5b7p7lN/eazn0wWvhR8\nHGqzE5oyNW1sE3KC3nJmDha/CQE2sGWNYC8IaET4poSVc8371HFSE3+RZo0LMlr9\nF+So7TJKjOgSmMxQ1fSui7cJaGRDoIlPv8hrLZQ+pRkCcAWGs3SqTIFIBKQDuQgU\nC8aAC0ISQhDlRIZlKSef\n=DF8+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T06:02:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDg4OGE2ZmE4ZjM1NzE1MzNjMjYwYjY1OGVjODA3M2NhMDFmNTNjMDA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21","sha":"aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21"}],"sha":"888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/888a6fa8f3571533c260b658ec8073ca01f53c00"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T21:14:54Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T21:14:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Count finished runs under the campaign root on either lane (#671)\n\n* Count finished runs under the campaign root on either lane\n\nA cluster campaign published no run progress: its status carried a batch total\nof zero and a progress that never moved, so the web UI''s run meter had no\ndenominator and painted nothing while jobs were running, and the controller\nlogged that the backend does not report finished runs.\n\nThe cluster backend''s finished-run count was deleted with the object store it\ncounted keys in, and nothing replaced it, so the base backend''s \"cannot count\"\nanswered for that lane. On both shipped lanes the campaign root is the campaign''s\ndurable home, and a cluster job''s uploader delivers a run''s results there before\nthe Job counts as complete -- the same files the local lane counts. The count of\nper-run result files under the root therefore moves from the Docker backend into\nthe base, where both lanes inherit it; a backend whose runs leave results where\nthe root cannot see them still overrides it, and one that cannot introspect still\nanswers None and is logged.\n\n* The run-counter note in future work names both lanes","tree":{"sha":"1955f010e7f7403fd2e6fdff51e2f9112699115f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/1955f010e7f7403fd2e6fdff51e2f9112699115f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21","verification":{"payload":"tree 1955f010e7f7403fd2e6fdff51e2f9112699115f\nparent 312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790198094 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790198094 +0200\n\nCount finished runs under the campaign root on either lane (#671)\n\n* Count finished runs under the campaign root on either lane\n\nA cluster campaign published no run progress: its status carried a batch total\nof zero and a progress that never moved, so the web UI''s run meter had no\ndenominator and painted nothing while jobs were running, and the controller\nlogged that the backend does not report finished runs.\n\nThe cluster backend''s finished-run count was deleted with the object store it\ncounted keys in, and nothing replaced it, so the base backend''s \"cannot count\"\nanswered for that lane. On both shipped lanes the campaign root is the campaign''s\ndurable home, and a cluster job''s uploader delivers a run''s results there before\nthe Job counts as complete -- the same files the local lane counts. The count of\nper-run result files under the root therefore moves from the Docker backend into\nthe base, where both lanes inherit it; a backend whose runs leave results where\nthe root cannot see them still overrides it, and one that cannot introspect still\nanswers None and is logged.\n\n* The run-counter note in future work names both lanes","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtEFOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAABLAQALCXiAKsXtN0GyYOqZHR7pi6\n89u4QTSsk30uhMJEkVDIBtMd6sKmdiVyyBy7EY/Tj9kDHPpo0li2uYe6JZYs2lIK\nHTjgWIZw8jOVa53+U3hEblx6LzDyp7v4I1qrmG90yCiTSB+k5euJe5A5JZ+lMEr9\n05BUqG9X15J4EV41DXz6YCBkusbzn2WAYtSkCuk2e3AF4MSsUdx8MA2M3FeAQ6oN\n70hiR+f+uZZDTv2MhAPwlQjgnDiaSoARjqQ7h9cvBDfRyaFbo/D6975sU1pMB5eY\ncG32Xpzd5+s/XfP2aena3XiCKl5HwFryq629YhBk0Zl9XtJBb8v4QX97NEhisxm7\nr4I8KkVI7fXvGWaMggyJJ8Nr+SfU7gMLBLTSQNCsD19r3lidX+tDQakWB9REd5tt\nMtw24/GOe6djWWxGqdamzW8zsETGRW1ZWM8rbQLmRMQZkuhZdwIBA9XLBAR1n8cD\nBuUElp4SFCh0oGGWP4T5ZxHRZEnX1zaK/d8ctFfa8yr/9kONwY4v9MRJvifBQ1Cb\n2VX5JZP+Wjve6T8a08Bp+HeVTBFtSsJ5OoFHtNdj0/Mnm2f0UFAQZcAGON2rPUkb\nezqwWar0s5cnIVCiKmBOn8ewGNe5is+CdcBpbcOM6TauJ9NMlfkkefW0vU6BZwL+\nTF5SZHO/uLzqxEvANyvv\n=pCcU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T21:14:54Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGFhZDhhZGM1Zjc0YmI4NmI2MTgyODU3ZGU5NzI0ZjVmOGMxYzdkMjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187","sha":"312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187"}],"sha":"aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/aad8adc5f74bb86b6182857de9724f5f8c1c7d21"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T21:03:54Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T21:03:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let the call record name its caller and report its own bounds (#442)\n\nAn `actor` column nothing writes is a capability the record advertises and does not have,\nand a page with no total reads as the whole log -- beside a ranking that summarises the\nretained window, so the two describe different sets with nothing saying which is narrower.\n\nEach row carries both answers to \"who called\". `actor` is the principal the service\nresolved: the name the caller gave and the source it authenticated by, which is what the\nservice knows about it. `session` is the client and its mcp-session-id, which is what\nseparates two agents sharing one token, and the principal cannot. Both are read before the\ncall, because the request context is gone by the time the record is written on a worker\nthread, and every lookup is guarded: accounting that raises is a way to break the call it\naccounts for.\n\nA read returns one page with the total it was cut from, the bounds applied to it and\nwhether more matched; `offset` reaches the rest, and one filter serves the count and the\npage so they can never describe different sets. The CSV export is bounded by what is\nretained rather than by a page ceiling, and a cut export says so in its filename, which is\nthe part of a saved file a reader still has later.\n\nThe table''s DDL is issued under the index''s `ddl_lock`, like every other writer''s: two\nservices reaching their first flush together both run it, and `IF NOT EXISTS` checks the\ncatalog and then creates rather than while it does.","tree":{"sha":"0746cd7a4e1d606960c31b9f4e52653bae288264","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/0746cd7a4e1d606960c31b9f4e52653bae288264"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187","verification":{"payload":"tree 0746cd7a4e1d606960c31b9f4e52653bae288264\nparent 970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790197434 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790197434 +0200\n\nLet the call record name its caller and report its own bounds (#442)\n\nAn `actor` column nothing writes is a capability the record advertises and does not have,\nand a page with no total reads as the whole log -- beside a ranking that summarises the\nretained window, so the two describe different sets with nothing saying which is narrower.\n\nEach row carries both answers to \"who called\". `actor` is the principal the service\nresolved: the name the caller gave and the source it authenticated by, which is what the\nservice knows about it. `session` is the client and its mcp-session-id, which is what\nseparates two agents sharing one token, and the principal cannot. Both are read before the\ncall, because the request context is gone by the time the record is written on a worker\nthread, and every lookup is guarded: accounting that raises is a way to break the call it\naccounts for.\n\nA read returns one page with the total it was cut from, the bounds applied to it and\nwhether more matched; `offset` reaches the rest, and one filter serves the count and the\npage so they can never describe different sets. The CSV export is bounded by what is\nretained rather than by a page ceiling, and a cut export says so in its filename, which is\nthe part of a saved file a reader still has later.\n\nThe table''s DDL is issued under the index''s `ddl_lock`, like every other writer''s: two\nservices reaching their first flush together both run it, and `IF NOT EXISTS` checks the\ncatalog and then creates rather than while it does.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtD66CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAs+AQAKzO6UmLaKsJtKWBUyjd49Yk\nuS8/Ll8hKJyB86DYypMF73qyUHYNG5ranPsEucvelTtUYD7aqfWqNz3dkKFxXk9o\ng4pml25+IEXKb7xaIQeZzf/InjpYfUENnNef+k04k/cIu1BnxMsiK0ax77HDjc49\n024ZAEM7s91tf7OFVEizI6bSLZ7pEL//GQ89IHh1R3GWDjPtS1/wp2CHUHf05RzD\nVZ8ArxooUzPZYpuTDbQkGJnyuiLOnWpALMfox2e+Hii7DFgMoVRyIVao6rmjr2i2\nIkTXHXpQQzErdRTuonI1wQLFnNHQ5ZMwkoUkZwVThVKMjIBQKA1mtQgtn36HAVVr\nseQ0lZWhvzZntQcTI9NHFMkekI9X3kvMKQGQ3kNhjXnwMYr9HuopBtkhhSAYD+g8\nX12OYJEL9AIfRFsbYtUyielvTh8yucQ5a4vHKB7VJEYb2Fs5QsD3p+01IHfFyegn\n9aw8XmxYCUyzbQAirdrJGIEpkoNdjgeOLs84Yz8WSGCDbpMG2IeLypVq94zfz2er\nCaWxgnzN5oA22Wpx5I752GtL+wQpdHJ5qJWN4c6TF3XQksYOv5KIddaD8J737uoi\ngi8S6cUfXjBz8++lnb3w4+MndgkmSWn6vYznlJ62gkQSwjsHIRf/5F309OR5D8WR\n/RYDd5+EZO0WF5UjDknI\n=q1cr\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T21:03:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDMxMjM1NGE4ODJlYzkzM2YwZmIxYTJhOTU0YTMyNzJjYTg5ZjExODc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4","sha":"970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4"}],"sha":"312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/312354a882ec933f0fb1a2a954a3272ca89f1187"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:55:24Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:55:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"nav_search runs again on the current simulator, and the costmap video overlay draws what the map2d panel declares (#589)\n\n* The nav_search barrier states its pose the way the box plugin reads it\n\nThe simulator''s box plugin takes a placement as `pose: {position: {x, y, z}}`, and a\ntwo-element `pos` is refused at config validation -- before a trial starts, and for every\ncampaign in this example, since the grid''s fixed instance list and the doorway variation\nevery search campaign composes its walls through both emit the placement. Project\nvalidation cannot see it: the base world declares the barrier empty, and only the\nvariation output carries the segments.\n\nBoth write the pose shape, with z omitted so a segment sits on the floor as a two-element\n`pos` means, and the grid''s notebook reads the gap back from it.\n\n* The costmap video overlay draws what the map2d panel declares\n\nA goal that is a scenario constant rather than a varied parameter reached neither the\nconfig view nor the video: no variation contributes it, and the overlay drew only what\nvariations contribute. The web UI already has the answer -- a geometry panel''s declared\nmarkers, resolved in the browser against the configuration -- but nothing outside the\nbrowser could resolve one.\n\n`robovast.common.panel_bindings.declared_markers` is that resolver in Python, with the\npanel kit''s semantics: a literal `pos`, `param:` from the configuration''s parameters,\n`internal:` from a variation''s `_` keys, `offset:` applied after, a `param:` the\nconfiguration lacks yielding nothing rather than a marker at the origin. The costmap\noverlay reads the campaign''s frozen `.vast` and draws its map2d panel''s declaration --\nthe map-frame one, which is the inset''s frame -- beside the contributed markers, labelled;\n`markers:` on the overlay takes the same declarations instead. The nav_search campaigns\ndeclare their start and goal on a map2d panel, so the config view shows them too.\n\nnumpy is allowed up to 3: the suite passes on 2, and a ceiling below it holds any\nenvironment that installs this beside a simulator built on 2 down to numpy 1. The lock\nis relocked with --no-update, so what is already installed does not move.\n\n* One pose reader for every marker, and declared markers arrive as SceneMarkers\n\nThe declared-marker resolver carried its own pose reader beside the one the nav\nconfig view already had, and handed back mappings that the overlay re-wrapped. Now\n`read_xy` / `read_pose` live with the marker vocabulary in core and the config view\nreads through them (a stated z is kept, as the browser''s reader keeps it); the resolver\nvalidates each entry as the DeclaredMarker the .vast was checked against and returns\nSceneMarkers, so there is no third shape between the declaration and the drawing. The\noverlay reads the campaign''s configurations file once.\n\n* A declared marker states its placement the way the rest of the file does\n\nA `.vast` states a placement as a pose everywhere else -- that is the shape the spawn\nservice and the box plugins read -- while a marker declared on a geometry panel could\nonly be written as a bare `pos` list with a separate `yaw`, so one file said the same\nthing two ways. `pose:` is now that spelling, and it carries the orientation with it, so\nstating one beside `pos`/`yaw` is refused rather than silently taking one of the two.\n`pos:` remains the bare-point form, which is what SceneMarker itself carries and what\nevery contributed marker is built from.\n\nBoth resolvers change together -- the panel kit''s, which the browser draws through, and\ncore''s, which the video overlay draws through -- because a declaration means one thing\nwherever it is read. The examples and the panel documentation show the pose form.\n\n* nav_search states the trial''s endpoints in the campaign, and the scenario only declares them\n\nThe scenario file carried default values for the start and goal, so a `.vast` that\nwanted them drawn had to repeat the numbers -- a marker that goes stale the moment the\nscenario changes, and a value nothing in the campaign record could vouch for. A scenario\ndescribes the procedure and its inputs; what a trial is given is the campaign''s to say.\n\nThe `.osc` now declares `start_pose` and `goal_pose` and states no value, each campaign\nstates them once under `parameters: scenario:`, and the map panel''s markers bind to them\nby name. The run, the config view and a video''s costmap inset then read the one value\nthe configuration carries; a parameter a configuration lacks draws nothing, as before.\n\n* Pin the two declared-marker cases the browser''s twin already pins\n\n`declared_markers` is the Python twin of the panel kit''s resolver, and two implementations of one semantics drift where only one of them is tested. The browser''s suite covers an `internal:` holding a pose and an `internal:` the configuration lacks; the Python suite covered the path form alone. Both now pin the same answer: a marker named after the key it read, and nothing drawn for a key that is not there.\n\n* Keep the endpoints'' explanation where the endpoints are declared\n\nEleven campaigns state the same two poses, because each says what it is run with. They\ncarried the same six lines about them as well, byte for byte, including the co-registration\nof empty_room''s map with its world -- which scenario_roqsim.osc states beside the parameters\nit declares, so eleven copies were a second source to disagree with it. The `.vast` files\npoint at it instead.\n\nThe panel comment said the endpoints are \"declared here, once\" in each of the eleven files.\nIt says what it means now: they are constants of the scenario rather than factors, so no\nvariation contributes them, and binding by name is what makes the config view and a video''s\ncostmap overlay read one value.","tree":{"sha":"598fc322e5127a30d725f1c7fe39694f7b0c3046","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/598fc322e5127a30d725f1c7fe39694f7b0c3046"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4","verification":{"payload":"tree 598fc322e5127a30d725f1c7fe39694f7b0c3046\nparent a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790196924 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790196924 +0200\n\nnav_search runs again on the current simulator, and the costmap video overlay draws what the map2d panel declares (#589)\n\n* The nav_search barrier states its pose the way the box plugin reads it\n\nThe simulator''s box plugin takes a placement as `pose: {position: {x, y, z}}`, and a\ntwo-element `pos` is refused at config validation -- before a trial starts, and for every\ncampaign in this example, since the grid''s fixed instance list and the doorway variation\nevery search campaign composes its walls through both emit the placement. Project\nvalidation cannot see it: the base world declares the barrier empty, and only the\nvariation output carries the segments.\n\nBoth write the pose shape, with z omitted so a segment sits on the floor as a two-element\n`pos` means, and the grid''s notebook reads the gap back from it.\n\n* The costmap video overlay draws what the map2d panel declares\n\nA goal that is a scenario constant rather than a varied parameter reached neither the\nconfig view nor the video: no variation contributes it, and the overlay drew only what\nvariations contribute. The web UI already has the answer -- a geometry panel''s declared\nmarkers, resolved in the browser against the configuration -- but nothing outside the\nbrowser could resolve one.\n\n`robovast.common.panel_bindings.declared_markers` is that resolver in Python, with the\npanel kit''s semantics: a literal `pos`, `param:` from the configuration''s parameters,\n`internal:` from a variation''s `_` keys, `offset:` applied after, a `param:` the\nconfiguration lacks yielding nothing rather than a marker at the origin. The costmap\noverlay reads the campaign''s frozen `.vast` and draws its map2d panel''s declaration --\nthe map-frame one, which is the inset''s frame -- beside the contributed markers, labelled;\n`markers:` on the overlay takes the same declarations instead. The nav_search campaigns\ndeclare their start and goal on a map2d panel, so the config view shows them too.\n\nnumpy is allowed up to 3: the suite passes on 2, and a ceiling below it holds any\nenvironment that installs this beside a simulator built on 2 down to numpy 1. The lock\nis relocked with --no-update, so what is already installed does not move.\n\n* One pose reader for every marker, and declared markers arrive as SceneMarkers\n\nThe declared-marker resolver carried its own pose reader beside the one the nav\nconfig view already had, and handed back mappings that the overlay re-wrapped. Now\n`read_xy` / `read_pose` live with the marker vocabulary in core and the config view\nreads through them (a stated z is kept, as the browser''s reader keeps it); the resolver\nvalidates each entry as the DeclaredMarker the .vast was checked against and returns\nSceneMarkers, so there is no third shape between the declaration and the drawing. The\noverlay reads the campaign''s configurations file once.\n\n* A declared marker states its placement the way the rest of the file does\n\nA `.vast` states a placement as a pose everywhere else -- that is the shape the spawn\nservice and the box plugins read -- while a marker declared on a geometry panel could\nonly be written as a bare `pos` list with a separate `yaw`, so one file said the same\nthing two ways. `pose:` is now that spelling, and it carries the orientation with it, so\nstating one beside `pos`/`yaw` is refused rather than silently taking one of the two.\n`pos:` remains the bare-point form, which is what SceneMarker itself carries and what\nevery contributed marker is built from.\n\nBoth resolvers change together -- the panel kit''s, which the browser draws through, and\ncore''s, which the video overlay draws through -- because a declaration means one thing\nwherever it is read. The examples and the panel documentation show the pose form.\n\n* nav_search states the trial''s endpoints in the campaign, and the scenario only declares them\n\nThe scenario file carried default values for the start and goal, so a `.vast` that\nwanted them drawn had to repeat the numbers -- a marker that goes stale the moment the\nscenario changes, and a value nothing in the campaign record could vouch for. A scenario\ndescribes the procedure and its inputs; what a trial is given is the campaign''s to say.\n\nThe `.osc` now declares `start_pose` and `goal_pose` and states no value, each campaign\nstates them once under `parameters: scenario:`, and the map panel''s markers bind to them\nby name. The run, the config view and a video''s costmap inset then read the one value\nthe configuration carries; a parameter a configuration lacks draws nothing, as before.\n\n* Pin the two declared-marker cases the browser''s twin already pins\n\n`declared_markers` is the Python twin of the panel kit''s resolver, and two implementations of one semantics drift where only one of them is tested. The browser''s suite covers an `internal:` holding a pose and an `internal:` the configuration lacks; the Python suite covered the path form alone. Both now pin the same answer: a marker named after the key it read, and nothing drawn for a key that is not there.\n\n* Keep the endpoints'' explanation where the endpoints are declared\n\nEleven campaigns state the same two poses, because each says what it is run with. They\ncarried the same six lines about them as well, byte for byte, including the co-registration\nof empty_room''s map with its world -- which scenario_roqsim.osc states beside the parameters\nit declares, so eleven copies were a second source to disagree with it. The `.vast` files\npoint at it instead.\n\nThe panel comment said the endpoints are \"declared here, once\" in each of the eleven files.\nIt says what it means now: they are constants of the scenario rather than factors, so no\nvariation contributes them, and binding by name is what makes the config view and a video''s\ncostmap overlay read one value.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDy8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHTsQAD6GvhcL2QBjmxU/Jsvto3dP\nRXVZuiZtG/SUKnGU5ntQXD+rdXdFRYs/T8729+hu1Fc6CEPadPKk5EswEgT7O6AL\nYNlN4JoRKFQCyWK5w3Gjnm8vqJzYXVA2iHf73feAXiV8vVHjxMVBJMUJIYxL8WgK\nhoK2xmxcJKtFVekNHtIfH8AGBGFp+URhAVUCrUVkMxYbuwgysEtFDOa4vx1QW8tm\nBIkHo05VKQyobiFJv51AxfVTpfzA9K/2iHOEU+OZvZFeHqcmZmNPw+4bGbHI2+N4\nryWQxecPsMvawSctcFB82wEY1KBHLz4muz0c/qZDwAm9RDUuLyDiTSdiP3/tEZL5\nf9IHEgTr+yQDCNZJx0H4npWME9o1tO0NKGgzMoEQYIz2aZ5nxJ1nhYQyKYpVAGQE\n6QmpwDgxu6N9zNfQ9BFIyHBCx/WOssSxMVtvm0kVd68LfCcgfwM9NQUdQpoTFaeW\niCgmoN8CnDxPrkmGqEAfT7GZq9MrZ4yERbXDbZ1rcmvb+s8qG/P/KEO36/go1e/F\nLWi1+3knZIaXg3vVuzB0sTVdoqFxRu/lYJSwil/wx6joORhmTf0v5/s3V/lly5Kh\nP8T2UNIFeG/NbNC75QzqTWL9TvnBVzdKfQaMIK/QX9wSiQXpnk+o+1NYIdkjRkR6\nt7+sNT1kOp0zIVhBsg9X\n=2B2T\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T20:55:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDk3MGM0NGNkOWE1NDQ1NDU1YTBkMjA4MDFhMWExMGRjMjAyYWE0ZTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59","sha":"a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59"}],"sha":"970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/970c44cd9a5445455a0d20801a1a10dc202aa4e4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:44:29Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:44:29Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"A workspace travels as an archive, over the same share as a campaign (#657)\n\n* A workspace travels as an archive, over the same share as a campaign\n\nOne archive serves all three directions a project moves in. `GET\n/workspaces/{id}/archive` streams the project tree under a single top-level directory;\n`POST /workspaces/{id}/share` publishes those same bytes to the configured share; and\n`create_workspace(from_share=...)` takes one back, always as a new workspace -- an archive\ncarries project files, not an identity, so there is nothing in it to restore or replace.\nFile by file is what a client can assemble and a browser cannot, and it moves nothing\nbetween deployments.\n\nThe share holds two grammars: `<campaign-id>.<variant>.tar.gz` and\n`<slug>.workspace.tar.gz`. They cannot collide, because a campaign id must still match\n`is_campaign_dir` once its token is taken off, and a workspace slug is read only from a\nname carrying the workspace token, which that pattern never leaves behind. Providers list\nevery archive and drop what is neither -- classifying one is the service''s job, so the\ngrammar has one implementation rather than four.\n\nExport and import are synchronous. A campaign''s upload is tracked as a phase of the\ncampaign and returns as soon as it is under way; a project tree has no such row to report\nprogress on, so the honest handle is the object that landed.\n\n`vast workspace download` fetches the archive rather than walking the file calls, which is\nthe same bytes in one request now that the endpoint exists for the browser.\n\nThreaded through every surface per docs/developer_guide.rst: the interface, both\ntransports, the route, the CLI, every `vast share` verb with `--workspace`, the MCP and the\nconfig editor.\n\n* Move a workspace from the config editor, and say so in the docs\n\nThree buttons beside the workspace picker: Download saves the project as a\ntar.gz, Export to share publishes it, Import from share brings one in as a new\nworkspace. The two share buttons are absent where the deployment has no share,\nbecause offering a verb the caller cannot perform is advertising a capability\nthat is not there.\n\nThe import dialog is its own component rather than a mode of the campaign one:\nthat dialog restores a campaign under its own id and reports the ones already\nhere, and every one of those branches is a question a workspace import does not\nask.\n\nAn archive rolled by hand carries `./` for its root. Read as the project''s\ndirectory that nests the whole tree one level down, and silently, since every\nfile still arrives -- so the prefix is stripped before the root is found, the\nway the campaign side strips it.\n\nThe MCP gains `export_workspace` and `from_share`, and no download tool: an\nagent reaches a workspace''s contents through the file tools over `/sources`,\nwhile a campaign''s archive is the gigabytes of rosbags no such tool should\nstream. The tool surface is at its token budget, so the neighbouring\ndescriptions were compressed to fit.","tree":{"sha":"51abea8df3dd4c217a1ea97b76246a36d25b3f85","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/51abea8df3dd4c217a1ea97b76246a36d25b3f85"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59","verification":{"payload":"tree 51abea8df3dd4c217a1ea97b76246a36d25b3f85\nparent 5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790196269 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790196269 +0200\n\nA workspace travels as an archive, over the same share as a campaign (#657)\n\n* A workspace travels as an archive, over the same share as a campaign\n\nOne archive serves all three directions a project moves in. `GET\n/workspaces/{id}/archive` streams the project tree under a single top-level directory;\n`POST /workspaces/{id}/share` publishes those same bytes to the configured share; and\n`create_workspace(from_share=...)` takes one back, always as a new workspace -- an archive\ncarries project files, not an identity, so there is nothing in it to restore or replace.\nFile by file is what a client can assemble and a browser cannot, and it moves nothing\nbetween deployments.\n\nThe share holds two grammars: `<campaign-id>.<variant>.tar.gz` and\n`<slug>.workspace.tar.gz`. They cannot collide, because a campaign id must still match\n`is_campaign_dir` once its token is taken off, and a workspace slug is read only from a\nname carrying the workspace token, which that pattern never leaves behind. Providers list\nevery archive and drop what is neither -- classifying one is the service''s job, so the\ngrammar has one implementation rather than four.\n\nExport and import are synchronous. A campaign''s upload is tracked as a phase of the\ncampaign and returns as soon as it is under way; a project tree has no such row to report\nprogress on, so the honest handle is the object that landed.\n\n`vast workspace download` fetches the archive rather than walking the file calls, which is\nthe same bytes in one request now that the endpoint exists for the browser.\n\nThreaded through every surface per docs/developer_guide.rst: the interface, both\ntransports, the route, the CLI, every `vast share` verb with `--workspace`, the MCP and the\nconfig editor.\n\n* Move a workspace from the config editor, and say so in the docs\n\nThree buttons beside the workspace picker: Download saves the project as a\ntar.gz, Export to share publishes it, Import from share brings one in as a new\nworkspace. The two share buttons are absent where the deployment has no share,\nbecause offering a verb the caller cannot perform is advertising a capability\nthat is not there.\n\nThe import dialog is its own component rather than a mode of the campaign one:\nthat dialog restores a campaign under its own id and reports the ones already\nhere, and every one of those branches is a question a workspace import does not\nask.\n\nAn archive rolled by hand carries `./` for its root. Read as the project''s\ndirectory that nests the whole tree one level down, and silently, since every\nfile still arrives -- so the prefix is stripped before the root is found, the\nway the campaign side strips it.\n\nThe MCP gains `export_workspace` and `from_share`, and no download tool: an\nagent reaches a workspace''s contents through the file tools over `/sources`,\nwhile a campaign''s archive is the gigabytes of rosbags no such tool should\nstream. The tool surface is at its token budget, so the neighbouring\ndescriptions were compressed to fit.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDotCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAARPsQAAeCXuwP9TlyILiiQrlW6MwV\nFEnNczfeNuxNL29Nrpw+jp3o7oiKsXiHISHUhbPL6gfwxtp+RuhO4Hq8cC36BLAa\ntVpZT0lZKmUJohtNaEOJbZLG+7Y6E7XBWGGg1fqnA1h9fYUQqeqZvUY7AoHQMihI\ntIqnbFGp+S8DIuhSVDsJWp1RJNJpDnAtZszcTYBzQwibh9AO2OzhkrTwy3QyfZvc\nNCwUVmYCU+sJ2TaBO/HDksDS/rIAoKzIZNrBnP5HYnGf8lfddjmUet+ZuNfIdwg1\nZH8sxfMiQEG/+7XwoAnM4LKpQQyXm8WghR0U+8cFZu5Uaa8JRnwppy+xl4QvVgJH\nd+V/OJc7uitfMyYAQhiL71dAbuTqC9/WL60lkQGd7oYDN7WFtsDSOnyUg5j46OAt\nil+miw6N7jhqMpY0fX0nNmfQyqejEtdiNUH+YTQxIhCKw1C1YIJcOyzuMMcRPNSI\n7bD292LeaW7pqUYkeHUDB8/52IMB+4AiReVdF0Gt/PE3WbCFfajnT/Kc1ShAq1Ar\noobRYEtyZW/QbRO/bK285KH+33u/VyQFxawY3klhksfhJaXOJ84QTQ15Gf+Yomf9\nf5HT34bzKSIhuhkPkYb7z7wucKwW17cuoFEqlgWC2ma+owh/LMrCwbJ+K9ZI4gyZ\niD1lRGeqWH8+tcL28Lba\n=brQj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T20:44:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGEyYjA2Njg2NWU3MjZiMDMxMmU3MzE3NjU0N2QyNmE1NzNhMDVhNTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286","sha":"5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286"}],"sha":"a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/a2b066865e726b0312e73176547d26a573a05a59"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:33:56Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:33:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Replace the service Deployment on upgrade, and fail a rollout whose volume cannot mount (#623)\n\n* Replace the service Deployment on upgrade, and fail a rollout whose volume cannot mount\n\nA live Deployment is replaced whole on conflict, not merged into. A strategic merge keys\n`volumes` by name and never drops one, so a deploy that runs without a credential it had\nconfigured before deletes the Secret while the pod template goes on mounting it. With the\nRecreate strategy the old pod is already gone, and the new one sits in ContainerCreating on\na FailedMount that nothing in the pod''s status reports -- the wait then spends the whole\n--timeout in silence with the API down. Replacing removes the volumes, the envFrom sources\nand the env vars a deploy no longer renders, along with anything set on the Deployment by\nhand.\n\nThe published origin is one such value, so it is recovered from the live Ingress when\nneither public_origin nor ingress_host is given: the Ingress is what the origin describes,\nand \"nobody states it\" then means the service is not published rather than that this deploy\ndid not know.\n\nThe rollout wait reads mount-failure Events for a pod placed on a node that has started\nnothing, and fails it after the grace window with the kubelet''s own message; its next step\nnames the volume and the CWD-only ./.env rule rather than container logs that do not exist.\nupgrade waits on wait_for_rollout alone, which already requires the new pod to be Available\nand is the wait that recognises a pod that will never start.\n\nThe failure message states that the service is down: under Recreate the previous pod is\nstopped before the new one is created, and telling a reader otherwise sends them to an API\nthat is not there.\n\n* A mount failure the pod got past does not block it\n\nAn Event outlives its cause. A Secret that syncs a moment after the pod is placed leaves a\nFailedMount behind, and while the image pulls the pod has the shape of one that will never\nstart: placed, nothing running. That Event read on its own fails a healthy pod once the\ngrace runs out, on the batch lane and in a service rollout alike.\n\nThe kubelet mounts every volume before it pulls an image or creates a container, so a\nPulling, Pulled, Created or Started Event at or after the last mount failure means the\nvolumes were set up. pod_volume_reason reads the pod''s own Events for that, only for a pod\nthat has a mount failure, and a read that fails keeps the failure: it is still the only\nevidence there is.\n\nThe four timestamp fields are two API types filled in by different writers, so the one this\ncompares is normalised to UTC-aware -- comparing an offset-carrying time with a naive one\nraises, and inside this comparison that would take down the wait that called it.","tree":{"sha":"21abe6f644506408e059ca4bb9428e3f5f7cbe61","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/21abe6f644506408e059ca4bb9428e3f5f7cbe61"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286","verification":{"payload":"tree 21abe6f644506408e059ca4bb9428e3f5f7cbe61\nparent c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790195636 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790195636 +0200\n\nReplace the service Deployment on upgrade, and fail a rollout whose volume cannot mount (#623)\n\n* Replace the service Deployment on upgrade, and fail a rollout whose volume cannot mount\n\nA live Deployment is replaced whole on conflict, not merged into. A strategic merge keys\n`volumes` by name and never drops one, so a deploy that runs without a credential it had\nconfigured before deletes the Secret while the pod template goes on mounting it. With the\nRecreate strategy the old pod is already gone, and the new one sits in ContainerCreating on\na FailedMount that nothing in the pod''s status reports -- the wait then spends the whole\n--timeout in silence with the API down. Replacing removes the volumes, the envFrom sources\nand the env vars a deploy no longer renders, along with anything set on the Deployment by\nhand.\n\nThe published origin is one such value, so it is recovered from the live Ingress when\nneither public_origin nor ingress_host is given: the Ingress is what the origin describes,\nand \"nobody states it\" then means the service is not published rather than that this deploy\ndid not know.\n\nThe rollout wait reads mount-failure Events for a pod placed on a node that has started\nnothing, and fails it after the grace window with the kubelet''s own message; its next step\nnames the volume and the CWD-only ./.env rule rather than container logs that do not exist.\nupgrade waits on wait_for_rollout alone, which already requires the new pod to be Available\nand is the wait that recognises a pod that will never start.\n\nThe failure message states that the service is down: under Recreate the previous pod is\nstopped before the new one is created, and telling a reader otherwise sends them to an API\nthat is not there.\n\n* A mount failure the pod got past does not block it\n\nAn Event outlives its cause. A Secret that syncs a moment after the pod is placed leaves a\nFailedMount behind, and while the image pulls the pod has the shape of one that will never\nstart: placed, nothing running. That Event read on its own fails a healthy pod once the\ngrace runs out, on the batch lane and in a service rollout alike.\n\nThe kubelet mounts every volume before it pulls an image or creates a container, so a\nPulling, Pulled, Created or Started Event at or after the last mount failure means the\nvolumes were set up. pod_volume_reason reads the pod''s own Events for that, only for a pod\nthat has a mount failure, and a read that fails keeps the failure: it is still the only\nevidence there is.\n\nThe four timestamp fields are two API types filled in by different writers, so the one this\ncompares is normalised to UTC-aware -- comparing an offset-carrying time with a naive one\nraises, and inside this comparison that would take down the wait that called it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDe0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA8+QQAJ6Iy8tRRTIc/GcYipMcLI/+\nNhFha1A0VjG+NXdgWjLnVfTOK6WjvwCBUPYI8RbWqOfgC9jNaO+Kj1aZQybIddgx\nGA817S4sEms2MYqL8yOYGfTGgvSuEAuI6waBy+g/2WqJLrfcNwBQ/t+LwlA7e8Kp\nyKz8FORjTfclNjscTLjEpa+cyULyQ6vnmi8LdKeRHfgJQMWe7ftuzApqCKiXGzAk\nZqeEXJFAXQh7LF/KDMOmyZCJw+bg/ZHlvy1nu3113Ze7pxSYwEb/xAHtFrdRhrVE\n6eOffDA60DC84pJWkMhAhQOuYxsAHRiEfCQphkJAohcSbBGsWr16Wyn8ShOAqgV9\nODZymLNkTHRCJAe3zpuzxGOt65nPlZt9qWHOAVP/foiQzVYrfRNpecVdZFoIKaMU\nUNzQH2FAHrEceKcjgVsFnP3f1iqo/HGFLvXVhrftjXXHNDHcP7ghBnyljS+8KthI\n0hphUsLshP53t8WXF82J1m//0kxeZhMyXgs+A3LAcKL0+z49ssZapZbOnxsrX9Gw\neZfLbcxd6mKeQq+H8owtRysvTbHsT57CojiM+NAl2LRbfugAJf3IFbiqrUmz+Rr0\np/wDryYvgT4w/DDQIFuP2Sn1KuTP5DOiOGhl1TddhvTF6uKsUlNhLImji28zaTBj\nbjQhAD5rtXb9/yEM9keF\n=7A7W\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T20:33:57Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKDVjNTFhMzcwNzI1NmU2MTY4NDY0OGFmYTI2MGExZWNlODJlNjAyODY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60","sha":"c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60"}],"sha":"5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/5c51a3707256e61684648afa260a1ece82e60286"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:27:33Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:27:33Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Refuse by name what a lane does not offer, and give the lanes one base (#663)\n\n* Refuse by name what a lane does not offer, and give the lanes one base\n\nTwo lanes run campaigns, and they do not offer the same operations: the\nlocal Docker lane runs one campaign at a time, so it has no queue for a\nrank or a hold to act on and no Deployment to roll; the cluster lane runs\nin pods, so it has no screen to open a window on. That asymmetry is fine.\nTwo things about it were not.\n\nA refusal did not read as one. A transport''s NotImplementedError reached\ncallers as a bare 500, and a lane''s own refusals were a ValueError, a\nRuntimeError or an ok=False sentence -- so \"this lane cannot\" arrived\nlooking like bad input, a conflict, or a bug, and never like an answer.\nUnsupportedOnLane is now the one exception for it: every implementation\ndeclares its LANE, the sentence names the operation and the lane, the app\nmaps it to 501 with unsupported_on_lane in the error header, and in\nprocess it is the same object -- so the CLI, the MCP tools, the web UI\nand a raw HTTP client all show one line.\n\nAnd a lane inherited the other''s answers. ClusterService subclassed\nLocalTransport, so whatever it did not override, a local body answered,\ninvisibly, until a caller reached it on the cluster. What the lanes share\nnow lives in ServiceBase and is correct for any lane; what differs is an\nabstract hook there, answered in each lane''s own class, and a lane that\nlacks one cannot be constructed. The base imports neither driver. Three\nthings that had been riding on the old shape go with it: a shutdown that\nreached docker rm -f behind a predicate the cluster happened to flip, a\nsibling-lane hook no lane ever implemented, and version and upgrade_info\ncomposing the cluster''s answer by correcting the local one.\n\nThe MCP tools that fell back to an in-process transport now do it through\nthe one function that spells the fallback out.\n\nPinned by tests: the sentence on all four surfaces, the lanes as siblings\nover the base with every hook answered, no driver named in a base body,\nthe local container teardown in the local class alone, and no refusal\nwritten on the base. Corrected in passing: the local lane does enforce a\ndeclared timeout, only an undeclared one is unbounded there; the 64 MiB\nshared-memory figure is Docker''s default rather than a lane''s; and two\ncomments named members that no longer exist.\n\n* Say which class a refusal is on each side of the wire\n\nIn process ``UnsupportedOnLane`` is what a caller holds; across HTTP it is the\n``ServiceError`` the transport builds from the status and the code header, and the code is\nwhat identifies it. The sentence is the same either way, which is the property the class\nexists for, but naming the class as the same one on both sides tells a caller to catch\nsomething it will never see.\n\nThe lane ledger states the shape it pins rather than the bugs that had it, and the local\nlane''s first method gets the blank line above it that every other one has.","tree":{"sha":"e683c66fbdea61566ca58757c900fcda703f97ff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/e683c66fbdea61566ca58757c900fcda703f97ff"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60","verification":{"payload":"tree e683c66fbdea61566ca58757c900fcda703f97ff\nparent dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790195253 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790195253 +0200\n\nRefuse by name what a lane does not offer, and give the lanes one base (#663)\n\n* Refuse by name what a lane does not offer, and give the lanes one base\n\nTwo lanes run campaigns, and they do not offer the same operations: the\nlocal Docker lane runs one campaign at a time, so it has no queue for a\nrank or a hold to act on and no Deployment to roll; the cluster lane runs\nin pods, so it has no screen to open a window on. That asymmetry is fine.\nTwo things about it were not.\n\nA refusal did not read as one. A transport''s NotImplementedError reached\ncallers as a bare 500, and a lane''s own refusals were a ValueError, a\nRuntimeError or an ok=False sentence -- so \"this lane cannot\" arrived\nlooking like bad input, a conflict, or a bug, and never like an answer.\nUnsupportedOnLane is now the one exception for it: every implementation\ndeclares its LANE, the sentence names the operation and the lane, the app\nmaps it to 501 with unsupported_on_lane in the error header, and in\nprocess it is the same object -- so the CLI, the MCP tools, the web UI\nand a raw HTTP client all show one line.\n\nAnd a lane inherited the other''s answers. ClusterService subclassed\nLocalTransport, so whatever it did not override, a local body answered,\ninvisibly, until a caller reached it on the cluster. What the lanes share\nnow lives in ServiceBase and is correct for any lane; what differs is an\nabstract hook there, answered in each lane''s own class, and a lane that\nlacks one cannot be constructed. The base imports neither driver. Three\nthings that had been riding on the old shape go with it: a shutdown that\nreached docker rm -f behind a predicate the cluster happened to flip, a\nsibling-lane hook no lane ever implemented, and version and upgrade_info\ncomposing the cluster''s answer by correcting the local one.\n\nThe MCP tools that fell back to an in-process transport now do it through\nthe one function that spells the fallback out.\n\nPinned by tests: the sentence on all four surfaces, the lanes as siblings\nover the base with every hook answered, no driver named in a base body,\nthe local container teardown in the local class alone, and no refusal\nwritten on the base. Corrected in passing: the local lane does enforce a\ndeclared timeout, only an undeclared one is unbounded there; the 64 MiB\nshared-memory figure is Docker''s default rather than a lane''s; and two\ncomments named members that no longer exist.\n\n* Say which class a refusal is on each side of the wire\n\nIn process ``UnsupportedOnLane`` is what a caller holds; across HTTP it is the\n``ServiceError`` the transport builds from the status and the code header, and the code is\nwhat identifies it. The sentence is the same either way, which is the property the class\nexists for, but naming the class as the same one on both sides tells a caller to catch\nsomething it will never see.\n\nThe lane ledger states the shape it pins rather than the bugs that had it, and the local\nlane''s first method gets the blank line above it that every other one has.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDY1CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAQEQAEiPVn0C7B8PNHDn36qLaqZ1\npvG+e1d5vMtKDSh6TzTPYmW/TqaqCIivCaRnIjZHAoNM1ZP+9EBexJezOWdHnfla\nZlIf7aitoqJisvorZ64q+m9yymGSmmljMNLuNizcp2w6mAfSr5UkTKB3BPPP5h7P\n1yAjq6hBxxS9cMgoPUqQ8OjDg9xBKPNyGaGHbhuOqoL3zombvf2VHTME/4zTUBZo\nxA0Xc4haq4vV7yblyL5h0TXMyvKFgtGy1IIhTgmucjWRMnSbbIcCaR2BrQ6Lb+ae\nqhRn5B4GIhXT6NmOinfDvSG7MH8BaH4Wd7zPohaXScOmguDEx/QaBAHuCkP64t9b\nY6ygBvsZy+5p2kGXz4m9Vems8tz9i4rY2gs0AFElxH0cfqFEh7tfzSfc2nhKV03d\nUCvmbLx6pd3ODs7/vbh8qo/GFAnETM/WABXJEcwAP41DgfNoxQ4aWl1HFP+MaG8H\noM9bkzDcg8FJsnqBcX7afZr7deKZn03w3mCCIv17GoKJtiPBeLlW75vN294IYifq\nuinN4Z2K9Vnh8d+fW041JrBNl2sqDw3+YnCg2B+UCFSr1j8tYYRFE7NEhsxQr+qw\nBx2B7MheWTQgXziOp/qVAuwMuIc3ABATSEQhYG3aXF6sRl4hP43+2YPfYVuGIavJ\nSHWyu9ez9cHVX2v6HN/i\n=pxvB\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T20:27:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60","node_id":"C_kwDOPVaL7doAKGM5NWViYmFiODc5ZWQzNDEyNzIxNDM2Y2JhZTA1MWM0ODAxZjNiNjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/cps-test-lab/robovast/commit/dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74","sha":"dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74"}],"sha":"c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/c95ebbab879ed3412721436cbae051c4801f3b60"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/83823539?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fred-labs","id":83823539,"login":"fred-labs","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzODIzNTM5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fred-labs","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/commits/dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:05:03Z","email":"fred-labs@mailbox.org","name":"fred-labs"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T20:05:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"A job pod downloads its own job documents, not every job''s (#665)\n\nThe composer writes a scenario-parameter and a simulator-override document per job into\n_transient/, and a pod reads one pair. The inputs route takes a required, repeatable\njob=<tag> and sends only the named tags'' documents; every other file there is campaign-wide\nand reaches every pod. Sending the whole directory makes each pod''s download and unpack grow\nwith the campaign''s job count, which on a large sweep is most of what it spends starting.\n\nA tag without a parameter document is refused with a 404 before any byte is streamed, a tag\nthat is not a single path segment with a 400, and a request naming no job with a 422.\n\nA scenario Job names its own tag. The manifest a calibration probe is derived from also\nnames the probe tags, since a probe reads its own parameter document and its base job''s\nsimulator document -- and only the tags this batch wrote a document for. The node set is\nlive, so the list read when the probes start is not necessarily the one the documents were\nwritten from, and one manifest serves every probe: a tag the route cannot answer for would\nlose the whole batch''s probes rather than leave the one node unmeasured. That node is\nmeasured by the next batch, which writes its document before it asks for it.\n\njob_documents() in campaign_archive is the one place the two file names are made: both\nlanes'' writers, the pod''s paths, the route''s filter and the reserved config-mount patterns\nall derive from it.","tree":{"sha":"ed499d740a9693ee96586b44a21eae54a779944c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/trees/ed499d740a9693ee96586b44a21eae54a779944c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cps-test-lab/robovast/git/commits/dc1c076da0c398762ae56ff0e77e067ff043cd74","verification":{"payload":"tree ed499d740a9693ee96586b44a21eae54a779944c\nparent b5b1c0eee83492caf1eeff7bcfc8bf4a0c048640\nauthor fred-labs <fred-labs@mailbox.org> 1790193903 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790193903 +0200\n\nA job pod downloads its own job documents, not every job''s (#665)\n\nThe composer writes a scenario-parameter and a simulator-override document per job into\n_transient/, and a pod reads one pair. The inputs route takes a required, repeatable\njob=<tag> and sends only the named tags'' documents; every other file there is campaign-wide\nand reaches every pod. Sending the whole directory makes each pod''s download and unpack grow\nwith the campaign''s job count, which on a large sweep is most of what it spends starting.\n\nA tag without a parameter document is refused with a 404 before any byte is streamed, a tag\nthat is not a single path segment with a 400, and a request naming no job with a 422.\n\nA scenario Job names its own tag. The manifest a calibration probe is derived from also\nnames the probe tags, since a probe reads its own parameter document and its base job''s\nsimulator document -- and only the tags this batch wrote a document for. The node set is\nlive, so the list read when the probes start is not necessarily the one the documents were\nwritten from, and one manifest serves every probe: a tag the route cannot answer for would\nlose the whole batch''s probes rather than leave the one node unmeasured. That node is\nmeasured by the next batch, which writes its document before it asks for it.\n\njob_documents() in campaign_archive is the one place the two file names are made: both\nlanes'' writers, the pod''s paths, the route''s filter and the reserved config-mount patterns\nall derive from it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtDDvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjhcQAEZQT+9aHHvCdw2HgtUKEpTV\nN9uOEp8OlYGgkgxEaARH4Mgih7SEBjuunwoIIog39JubdKC7jnA22dkw4+ApmRfo\nxFekiRYMEiBT0FVrWN78eFGMs/Z+m9DV9d4AvvnLUMGrFea7//oGCkYgbabY/b1Z\nQSnLRWJQ85m94mu9m//5XUMsXRcKNEuGnafCrHoTWdvd49dmUP7ltcfGvTQsopIi\naHyZPmBAdJ7pQRuzay7A1TQ3pRXXzNcv1Diy5/kzgdhPMlYu1eaBi63OjjTX7xI9\nHphJ2VWXNmnv6oh5wLYGAg3GtwZKykPEsw9Wa7nk9wg79aifvU7ErAuKl2QwdEgV\nc6/kAHuJqTSANTZMotPCPCA/aaGIqc8nRa/wuOYZeqTfl5J5lrP+xEyBjmPoJ4H9\n+XuVAY2lp1KMz4wb6Lk2WcHHSsyTA3pqjlLS8TlHm62NEuF5S9oFC+FbELJFtjLR\nBoN3Q83mBHC5AGJna4tgDba9XjT5ow05/48KN0H7Gz1LReJ9acQvU89WDa2qLybh\n22JvXtCnKygEHmebX7J2OXGAsGR9vg69uoNlIBJkluz